# Cyrius Field Notes — RESOLVED Traps (retro)
# Entries retired from `../gotchas.cyml` at the v6.6.1 audit.
#
# WHAT IS IN HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT IN gotchas.cyml
# --------------------------------------------------
# Each of these is a REAL finding, kept verbatim. What they are not is a trap that
# can still bite someone working on cyrius today. Two shapes dominate:
#
#   1. A specific bug in a specific release, fixed, in code that has since been
#      restructured — the v5.11.x kernel/PE/EFI cluster, the Mach-O byte-array and
#      TS-parser fixes, the aarch64 epoll reactor.
#   2. A CHANGE RECORD wearing a gotcha's clothes — "v6.3.8 added lexical capture",
#      "v6.4.51 raised output_buf to 1 GiB", "v6.4.0 flipped monomorph default-on".
#      Those belong to the CHANGELOG and to the feature entries in
#      `content/cyrius/language/features.cyml`, not to a file you consult BEFORE
#      touching the compiler.
#
# ⛔ THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS. gotchas.cyml had reached 158 entries / 5,566 lines
# / 461 KB by being treated as append-only. A gotchas file nobody can read is worth
# less than no gotchas file, because it still costs a search and returns noise. The
# v6.6.1 audit cut it to 105 live traps; these 44 moved here instead of being
# deleted, per the repo rule: ARCHIVE, DO NOT DELETE.
#
# ⚠ Before re-promoting anything from this file, verify against LIVE code that the
# trap is reachable again. An entry landing back in gotchas.cyml should be a
# measured regression, not a hunch.
#
# 44 entries.
[[entries]]
name = "slice_fn_local_layout_flip"
description = "Pre-§8 16-byte fn-local slices put the canonical slot at the higher stack address; `&slice + 8` overflowed past the local frame into saved-rbp territory. §8 layout-flip (v5.8.16) allocates the high-half slot FIRST (higher address) and the canonical slot SECOND (lower address)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["slices", "layout", "stack", "v5.8.16"]
content = """
**The trap**: pre-v5.8.16, `var s: [u8] = 0;` allocated a
single canonical slot (the named slot, where `&s` resolves)
and a sentinel-cleared high-half slot. The canonical slot was
the LOWER-numbered slot (higher stack address per cyrius's
slot-N-at-rbp-(N+1)*8 convention). `slice_set(&s, ptr, len)`
did `store64(s+8, len)`, which on the stack means
`store64(rbp - canon_disp + 8, len)` — overflowing past the
local frame into saved-rbp territory.

This corrupted the caller's saved rbp. Worked anyway:

  1. Round-tripped within the fn — slice_set wrote and slice_len
     read the same (corrupted) location.
  2. `slices_typing.tcyr`'s `return slice_len(&x);` form passed
     because main's rbp = 0 from the C runtime — the read
     returned 0 by coincidence.
  3. §7 indexing tests passed because each test exited via
     `syscall(60, ...)` before any code referenced the corrupted
     rbp.

**Surfaced when**: §8 introduced `s.len` reads via &s+8
(memory I/O through the canonical-slot address) — same
arithmetic as slice_set, but the test reading expected a
deterministic value, not "whatever happened to be at saved rbp."

**Fix**: for `scalar_type==16`, allocate the HIGH-HALF slot
FIRST (slot li, higher address) and the CANONICAL slot SECOND
(slot li+1, lower address). The name + meta live on slot li+1
so FINDLOCAL returns it; EFLADDR(li+1) yields the lower
address; EFLADDR(li+1) + 8 lands on slot li (higher address) —
matching the slice convention's "second 8 bytes."

Init both halves: zero the canonical slot via EFLSTORE(li+1)
and zero the high half via address-based memory store
(EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ESTOC). Address-based init for
the high half is required because the regalloc post-body
peephole would patch EFLSTORE(li) to `mov rbx, rax` (writing
to a register, not the stack slot). See `slice_high_half_regalloc_pin`.

**Recognize again**: any "fn-local 16-byte type silently fails"
shape. If two adjacent `var x: u128` or `var s: [T]` interact
weirdly, check the layout-flip is still in place.

Full backstory: v5_8.cyml's `slice_fn_local_layout_flip_v5816`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "slice_high_half_regalloc_pin"
description = "Slice's high-half slot has no user-visible name; the regalloc post-body peephole would allocate a callee-saved register for it, leaving the actual stack slot uninitialized. §8 routes high-half I/O through address-based memory access to keep the slot in memory."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["slices", "regalloc", "v5.8.16"]
content = """
**The trap**: the regalloc post-body peephole (parse_fn.cyr
v5.6.20 picker) counts `mov [rbp+disp], rax` /
`mov rax, [rbp+disp]` patterns per slot to decide which user-
locals to pin to callee-saved registers (rbx, r12-r15). Time-
sliced sharing assumes every local interval starts with a STORE —
the register holds the right value from the start of each
interval, no LOAD-from-stack needed.

The slice's high-half slot has no user-visible name (sentinel-
cleared by the §8 layout-flip), so nothing else takes its
address via `&<ident>`. The picker's safety scan bars
`lea rax, [rbp+disp]` (treats `&local` as not-safe). Without
intervention, the high-half slot looks mov-only — picker
allocates rbx for it.

Then `var s: [T] = 0;`'s init `EFLSTORE(li, rax=0)` patches to
`mov rbx, rax` — writes 0 to rbx, leaves the stack slot
uninitialized. Subsequent slice_set memory-writes to the slot
are invisible to the register; `s.len` reads (which go through
the memory access path) see uninit stack data.

**Fix**: init the high half via address-based memory store
(EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ESTOC) rather than EFLSTORE.
The picker doesn't count memory-via-rcx writes, so the high
slot stays in memory. §8 dot-syntax (.len read/write) and
slice_len helper-fn calls all see the right value.

Same reasoning for §8 dot-syntax `s.len`: lower to address-
based load (EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ELODC), not direct
EFLLOAD against the slot. `s.ptr` could safely use EFLLOAD
(canonical slot is naturally barred from regalloc by slice_set
taking `&s`), but uses address-based for symmetry.

**Recognize again**: any "no-name auxiliary slot interacts
weirdly with regalloc" shape. The picker's safety scan only
bars slots with `&` taken via a NAMED ident; phantom slots
(high half of a 16-byte type, scratch slots) need explicit
exclusion.

Full backstory: v5_8.cyml's `slice_high_half_regalloc_pin_v5816`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "distlib_marker_styles_v5865"
description = "Two distlib-bundle marker styles exist in cyrius's vendored lib/: `# Generated by: cyrius distlib` (cyrius distlib output — patra/sigil/vani/yukti/sankoch/agnosys/mabda/sandhi) and `# Bundled distribution of <name>` (sakshi only — its scripts/bundle.sh predates cyrius distlib). audit-walk + ci.yml's stdlib walkers must skip BOTH."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "audit-walk", "fmt", "lint", "sakshi", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`'s
`_aw_is_distlib_bundle()` and the inline equivalents in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s `Format check (stdlib)` /
`Lint (stdlib)` / `Doc coverage (stdlib)` steps used to match
exactly one marker:
```sh
head -n 5 "$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$"
```
When sakshi was folded at v5.8.65, its dist file's header
read:
```
# sakshi.cyr — tracing, error handling, and structured logging
# Bundled distribution of sakshi v2.2.3
```
sakshi builds its dist via its own `scripts/bundle.sh`
predating cyrius's `cyrius distlib` subcommand. Different
header style. The skip-check didn't match → audit-walk ran
`cyrfmt --check` against the vendored bundle, found drift in
some indented `#` comment lines (sakshi-side bundle.sh produces
output whose indent style differs from current cyrfmt's
canonical), and the Format gate failed.

**Wrong fix would be**: apply `cyrfmt --write lib/sakshi.cyr`.
That would make lib/sakshi.cyr DIVERGE from sakshi's
dist/sakshi.cyr — breaking the byte-identity guarantee that's
the whole point of the foldin.

**Right fix**: extend the marker to recognize both styles:
```sh
head -n 5 "$f" 2>/dev/null | \
    grep -qE "^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$|^# Bundled distribution of "
```
Updated in 4 places at v5.8.65: `scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`'s
`_aw_is_distlib_bundle` + 3 inline call sites in ci.yml
(Format/Lint/Doc-coverage stdlib steps). Mirror comment in
ci.yml notes the audit-walk equivalence so future readers know
they're paired.

**Recognize again**: any new sibling-fold whose dist generator
isn't cyrius distlib needs the marker check audited. The first
few lines of the fold-target's dist file should announce the
generator clearly. If neither `Generated by: cyrius distlib`
nor `Bundled distribution of` appears in the first 5 lines,
add the third marker style at all 4 call sites.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_deps_global_symlink_wedge_v51117"
description = "cyrius deps stdlib_dir resolved from ~/.cyrius/lib (mutable global symlink) before v5.11.17 — wedge for the snapshot-ping-pong wipe"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "per-repo-isolation", "v5.11.17", "snapshot-ping-pong"]
---
Surfaced 2026-05-11 at v5.11.3 ship and again at v5.11.13.
Sibling agents (agnosys, mabda, phylax) run `cyriusly use
<other-version>` to test against pinned toolchains; this
flips `~/.cyrius/current` + the `~/.cyrius/bin` /
`~/.cyrius/lib` symlinks. Concurrent `cyrius deps` resolution
in this repo read from the stale snapshot and copied OLD
lib/*.cyr files BACK over in-flight edits.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

`cbt/deps.cyr::_dep_find_stdlib_dir()` resolution order
pre-v5.11.17:

  1. `~/.cyrius/lib` (the global symlink — mutable by ANY
     sibling agent on the dev box)
  2. `./lib` dev-mode fallback

For the cyrius source repo, step 1 ALWAYS won — even though
`./lib` was the actual source of truth. Any sibling-agent
version flip mid-edit triggered the copy-back.

== FIX (v5.11.17) ==

Resolution order rewritten:

  (a) `./lib` if `src/main.cyr` exists (cyrius-repo signal —
      dev-mode preferred; closes the cyrius-repo ping-pong
      since the repo reads from its own in-tree files)
  (b) `~/.cyrius/versions/<cyml-cyrius-field>/lib/` if
      cyrius.cyml's `[package].cyrius = "X.Y.Z"` field is
      set AND that version is installed (per-repo isolation
      for downstream consumers)
  (c) `~/.cyrius/lib` legacy fallback for repos without a
      cyrius pin

Plus: pinned-but-not-installed produces a hard error
(`error: cyrius.cyml pins version X.Y.Z but it is not
installed at <path>; run: cyrius install X.Y.Z`) — never
silent slide to `latest`.

== DETECTION ==

If lib/*.cyr edits revert mid-session AFTER v5.11.17 ship,
investigate:

  1. `cat ~/.cyrius/current` — if not matching `cat VERSION`,
     a sibling agent flipped it (recovery: `cyriusly use
     <correct-version>`).
  2. `find ~/Repos -maxdepth 2 -type l -name lib` — finds
     directory-level lib symlinks (the bad pattern; should
     return empty).
  3. `grep '^cyrius =' cyrius.cyml` — confirms which version
     the repo pins for deps resolution.

Pre-v5.11.17 the recovery dance was: copy lib snapshot back,
re-edit, refresh install snapshot, run check.sh. Post-fix:
just recovery for the `current` symlink flip; lib edits
themselves are protected.

== RELATED ==

See `snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption` (v5.8.26 surface) for
the full ping-pong shape. v5.11.17 closes one leg of the
ping-pong; v5.11.24 (per-repo isolation Part 2 — CLI version-
resolved dispatcher) and v5.11.25 (Part 3 — `cyriusly use
--global` flag) will close the others.

[[entries]]
name = "parse_nested_call_string_quirk_var_stage_hint_v51128"
description = "expected ')', got string on nested 2-arg fn-calls inside assert — diagnostic now hints at var-stage workaround"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "diagnostic", "fn-args", "var-stage", "v5.11.28", "bote", "v5.11.18"]
---
bote 2.7.1 filed an intermittent `expected ')', got string`
on the shape:

```cyrius
assert(streq(vec_get(caps_v, 0), "fetch") == 1, "msg")
```

i.e. `assert( fn( fn_with_2args(a, b) , LITERAL) == 1,
LITERAL )` — a 2-arg inner call inside another fn-call
inside `assert`. Filing was at cyrius 5.10.34. Trigger was
path-dependent: error position drifted line 187/184/178/173
as preceding lines changed, and after full chunk-by-chunk
re-add the error stopped reproducing — bote couldn't
reliably trigger it even at filing-time. Consumer
workaround: stage the inner call into a `var` first.

== PREMISE-CHECK AT v5.11.28 SLOT ENTRY ==

Reverted bote's `cap0/cap1` var-stage workaround to
recreate the inline-nested shape, compiled at both
v5.11.27 (current) AND v5.10.34 (filing version, intact
at `~/.cyrius/versions/5.10.34/bin/cc5`): clean parse
both times. Synthetic fuzz 0/28 triggers across 9
preceding-line counts (50→240 SSRF-URL asserts before
the trigger) × both versions AND 10 ident counts
(100→10000 filler fns spanning the filing's ~8700
speculation boundary). No reproduction obtainable.

== LIKELY SILENT FIX ==

Commit `f3e98a3e` (first in v5.11.18) doubled the
identifier buffer 131072 → 262144 bytes. This lines up
with the filing's Speculation 2 ("hash collision or
scan-window boundary at ~8700 idents") — capacity
doubling shifts hash-bucket distribution and probe-window
boundaries. Not proof-grade (can't verify without a
repro), but the timing fits the disappearance window.

== WHAT v5.11.28 SHIPS ==

(1) `tests/tcyr/parse_nested_call_assert.tcyr` (13 asserts)
locks the nested-call shape — bote's exact trigger,
3-deep nested variant, and the same shape preceded by a
7-assert SSRF-URL + JSON-literal stress block
approximating bote's ~180-line preceding context. Future
parser refactors that reintroduce the state leak surface
in this file.

(2) `src/common/util.cyr:425-432` adds a hint to
`ERR_EXPECT` when surface is `expected ')'` or
`expected ','` AND got-token is a string literal:

```
error:<source>:N: expected ')', got string
  hint: a string literal where ')' is expected often means
        a nested call inside a fn argument confused the
        parser; stage the inner call into a `var` first.
```

The hint sits between the diagnostic line and the existing
capacity dump, so consumers see "stage to var" before they
start re-balancing quotes or escapes. The filing
explicitly called out the misleading-diagnostic burden
("the `expected ')', got string` symptom sends consumers
down the wrong rabbit hole") as the part worth fixing
even when the underlying bug is intermittent.

== LESSON ==

A misleading diagnostic earns its own slot-share even when
the underlying bug is unreproducible. The bote filing
spent ~45 minutes of bisect before the var-stage rescue
became obvious; one hint string saves that future-time
for every consumer whose parse fails this shape, whether
the original state-leak is still live or not.

[[entries]]
name = "elf64_kernel_fixup_entry_mismatch_v51146"
description = "FIXUP's kmode==1 hard-coded entry=0x100060 (ELF32+mb1 layout) while EMITELF64_KERNEL writes code at 0x1000A8 (ELF64+mb2 layout). 0x48-byte fixup shift produced garbled serial after GRUB-EFI handoff on agnos. Fix: gate FIXUP's kmode==1 entry on _TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "fixup", "elf64", "multiboot2", "agnos", "v5.11.43", "v5.11.46", "cross-emitter-invariant"]
---
**THE TRAP.** FIXUP's `kmode==1` path hard-coded `entry = 0x100060` (the ELF32 +
multiboot1 layout) while `EMITELF64_KERNEL` writes code at `0x1000A8` (ELF64 +
multiboot2). Every absolute fixup was therefore shifted by **0x48 bytes** — the
kernel booted and produced garbled serial output after the GRUB-EFI handoff, which
reads as a driver bug, not a linker one.

**FIX**: gate FIXUP's `kmode==1` entry value on `_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL`.

**RECOGNIZE AGAIN**: two emitters that must agree on a layout constant, where one
holds it as a literal. Any second ELF/PE/Mach-O container variant needs its entry
base DERIVED from the same helper the writer uses (`_entry_base`), never re-typed.

⟳ Full narrative in `retros/v511x.cyml` — compressed here at the v6.6.1 gotchas
audit, which found this file carrying ~500 lines of backstory the retro already had.
[[entries]]
name = "pe_entry_prologue_e9_jmp_zero_preserves_gpr_v51148"
description = "cyrius PE entry emits e9 00 00 00 00 (5-byte jmp +0) at AddressOfEntryPoint before user code. The jmp touches no GPRs, so RCX/RDX/R8/R9 (MS x64 first 4 args) survive into the first user instruction. Inline-asm-first is the right shape for capturing UEFI firmware-supplied args"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "entry-point", "ms-x64-abi", "uefi", "inline-asm", "v5.11.48", "register-preservation"]
---
While scoping `programs/efi_probe.cyr` at v5.11.48 entry,
the question was: where in cyrius's PE entry does user
code start, and do RCX/RDX (firmware-supplied
ImageHandle / SystemTable per the UEFI MS x64 ABI)
survive long enough for user code to capture them?

== EMPIRICAL ANSWER ==

Compiled a probe with a distinctive 4-NOP marker as
the FIRST user inline-asm:

```cyrius
kernel;
asm { 0x90; 0x90; 0x90; 0x90; }   # marker
asm { 0x48; 0x89; 0xC8; }          # mov rax, rcx
var result = 42;
```

Compiled with `CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1` and inspected
`.text` from the AddressOfEntryPoint:

```
.text first 24 bytes: e9 00 00 00 00 90 90 90 90 48 89 c8 b8 2a 00 00 00 48 b9 ...
                      └─ prologue ─┘ └─ 4 NOPs ─┘ └─ mov rax,rcx ─┘ └─ var init ...
```

The 4-NOP marker landed at `.text + 0x05` (after the
5-byte cyrius prologue `e9 00 00 00 00` = `jmp +0`).

== WHY THE PROLOGUE PRESERVES GPRS ==

`jmp rel32` writes only RIP — no flags, no GPRs, no
memory. Even when `disp32 == 0` (a 5-byte effective
no-op), it remains a register-preserving instruction.
So firmware-supplied RCX (ImageHandle), RDX
(SystemTable), R8, R9 (the MS x64 first 4 args, also
the UEFI calling convention) all survive intact into
the first byte of user code.

== WHY FN CALLS DO NOT PRESERVE GPRS ==

Cyrius's normal fn-call emit shape (MS x64 ABI under
`_TARGET_PE == 1`) emits arg-setup before the call:

```
mov rcx, <arg1>    ; clobbers ImageHandle
mov rdx, <arg2>    ; clobbers SystemTable
call <fn>
```

If user code calls *any* fn before capturing RCX/RDX,
the firmware-supplied values are lost. So "capture
RCX/RDX via inline asm before any fn call" is the
required entry shape for UEFI Applications written
in cyrius.

== THE EFI_PROBE PATTERN ==

`programs/efi_probe.cyr` (v5.11.48) uses a single
`asm { ... }` block as the entire program body —
no globals (no fixups → no DIR64 → minimal `.reloc`),
no fn calls (no GPR clobbering), runtime register-
indirect addressing only. The asm walks
`SystemTable → ConOut → OutputString` and embeds the
UTF-16 message bytes inline at the end of the block,
addressed via RIP-relative LEA with a hand-computed
displacement. ~30 bytes of code + 28 bytes of message
+ 1 NOP for 2-byte CHAR16 alignment.

This pattern is the lower-bound shape for any future
cyrius UEFI app that needs to keep firmware args
alive. gnoboot will use a heavier shape (it has
globals, fns, allocator) but the initial argument
capture has to be inline-asm-first.

== ALTERNATIVE NOT TAKEN ==

A "tinified fn prologue" approach (preserve RCX/RDX
via inline asm at entry, then call a regular fn with
the captured values) was considered but rejected:
inline-asm support for emitting a fixup-aware
`mov reg, &global` would require compiler work
(cyrius's `asm { 0xXX; }` byte-emit doesn't have a
fixup-record hook). For the .48 MVP probe, the
single-asm-block shape works because everything is
register-indirect. A future slot may add fixup-aware
inline asm if/when gnoboot needs it.

[[entries]]
name = "dll_characteristics_nx_compat_zero_fixup_efi_v51148"
description = "v5.6.31 set DllCharacteristics = 0x0160 (NX_COMPAT + DYNAMIC_BASE + HIGH_ENTROPY_VA) only when .reloc was present. Zero-fixup EFI binaries (probe-style) had DllChar = 0x0000 with no NX_COMPAT, which UEFI 2.0+ may reject. Fix: EFI mode forces NX_COMPAT bit (0x0100) regardless of .reloc presence"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "dll-characteristics", "nx-compat", "uefi", "v5.11.48", "efi-application", "memory-attributes-protocol"]
---
The v5.6.31 logic at `src/backend/pe/emit.cyr:776`:

```cyrius
var _dllc = 0;
if (_pe_reloc_vsize != 0) { _dllc = 0x0160; }
```

set DllCharacteristics to `0x0160` (NX_COMPAT 0x0100 +
DYNAMIC_BASE 0x0040 + HIGH_ENTROPY_VA 0x0020) **only**
when `.reloc` was present. For Windows-CUI consumers
with at least one global (almost always true), this
shipped the bits the security audits wanted.

For an EFI Application probe that uses no globals (no
DIR64 entries → no `.reloc` content → `_pe_reloc_vsize
== 0`), DllCharacteristics = `0x0000`. UEFI 2.0+
firmware honors NX on code pages via the Memory
Attributes Protocol; without `NX_COMPAT` (0x0100) set,
some firmware implementations refuse to invoke the
image (mark `.text` as no-execute and trap on entry).

== FIX SHAPE (v5.11.48) ==

```cyrius
var _dllc = 0;
if (_pe_reloc_vsize != 0) { _dllc = 0x0160; }
if (_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION == 1 && _pe_reloc_vsize == 0) {
    _dllc = 0x0100;   # NX_COMPAT only
}
```

EFI mode + zero fixups now forces NX_COMPAT alone.
DYNAMIC_BASE stays off because the image is
non-relocatable (no `.reloc`); firmware honors
ImageBase = 0x140000000 directly. EFI programs with
at least one global fixup get the full `0x0160` from
the existing v5.6.31 branch (.reloc present →
relocatable → DYNAMIC_BASE meaningful → HIGH_ENTROPY_VA
meaningful too).

== CROSS-REFERENCE ==

This is the **second** silent-rejection class hit by
the v5.11.49 OVMF runtime smoke. The first was
RELOCS_STRIPPED (see `relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149`
entry above). Both required runtime testing to surface
— structural inspection alone would have missed both.

== HIGH_ENTROPY_VA NOTE ==

The v5.5.35 comment block in `pe/emit.cyr` documents
that HIGH_ENTROPY_VA (0x0020) was empirically observed
to break cc5_win.exe's stdin read on Win11 (5/5 runs).
That failure does NOT apply under UEFI — firmware
doesn't have stdin in the Windows sense; OutputString
+ runtime services are unaffected. The `0x0160` for
EFI mode with `.reloc` present is safe; the Win11
stdin issue is Windows-only.

[[entries]]
name = "build_cc5_silent_contamination_recurrence_v51144"
description = "build/cc5 has been silently overwritten with non-cyrius binaries multiple times across v5.11.x — at v5.11.28/.29 (silently fixed .30) and again at v5.11.43 (a 1.39 MB binary containing mabda/wgpu/compute-pipeline strings). install.sh --refresh-only carries the contamination into ~/.cyrius/bin/cc5. v5.11.45 added a grep gate"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "build", "contamination", "release-process", "v5.11.43", "v5.11.44", "v5.11.45", "mabda", "wgpu", "fail-fast-gate"]
---
v5.11.44 caught a P0 build artifact: the committed
`build/cc5` at v5.11.43 (commit `aad19f6a` EMIT64KERNEL)
was a **1,389,776 B binary containing mabda/wgpu/
compute-pipeline strings — NOT the cyrius compiler**.

`git diff aad19f6a -- src/ lib/` was empty;
current `src/` self-hosted byte-identical to the
canonical 821,712 B at v5.11.43 / 821,984 B at .44.
v5.11.41's cc5 fed current `src/main.cyr` also produced
821,712 B. So the source was clean; only `build/cc5`
was wrong.

== RECURRENCE PATTERN ==

Same shape contamination at v5.11.28/.29, silently
fixed at .30 with no postmortem at the time. Recurred
at .43 → caught at .44.

Most likely cause: a build script during the .43
prep cp'd another binary (a mabda-built binary that
happened to be in the build directory or in a sibling
repo's `build/`) over `build/cc5` immediately before
the commit. `install.sh --refresh-only` then carried
the contamination into `~/.cyrius/bin/cc5` +
`~/.cyrius/versions/5.11.43/bin/cc5` (both 1,389,776 B
mabda carryovers verified at .44 ship).

== WHY IT'S HARD TO DETECT NORMALLY ==

The contamination is invisible to `cyrius test` and
self-host verify if you happen to run them through
the OLD cc5 in `~/.cyrius/bin/`. The new
`build/cc5` is what's broken, but the install-snapshot
shim might re-symlink to a previous-version's cc5
if its mtime check skips the rebuild. Layered
silent-fail until somebody actually pipes
`src/main.cyr` through the committed `build/cc5`.

== FIX (v5.11.45) ==

New `_cc5_contamination_gate()` in
`programs/check.cyr` (v5.11.45 P(-1) hardening bundle).
Greps `build/cc5` for `mabda: gpu` / `wgpuDevice` /
`compute_pipeline` substrings — the v5.11.43-style
slip signature. Fails check.sh fast. Negative test
verified: tainting `build/cc5` makes the gate FAIL;
restoring → PASS.

== PROCESS PIN (CLOSEOUT-CANDIDATE) ==

The CLAUDE.md closeout pass should include a
`strings build/cc5 | grep -E "mabda|wgpu|compute_pipeline"`
check before any version-bump commits. v6.0.0
closeout will be the natural place to lift this from
"gate that catches it after commit" to "pre-commit
hook that refuses to commit a contaminated cc5".

== ADJACENT SLIP: cyrius vet AT v5.11.45 ==

The same v5.11.45 P(-1) hardening bundle caught a
parallel slip in `build/cyrc`: had been silently
replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary instead of
the real audit tool. install.sh `_rebuild_stale`
skipped the rebuild because the bad binary's mtime
was newer than source — same shape of silent-fail
as build/cc5's mabda contamination. Fixed by adding
an explicit `syscall(60, r)` exit to
`programs/cyrc.cyr` (vet was running twice — module
init + runtime fall-through), and raising the
scan-buffer cap 16 KB → 256 KB (was truncating reads
on 86 KB source files and falsely reporting "no
dependencies"). cyrc 12,344 B (bootstrap) → 44,672 B
(real audit tool).

== LESSON ==

`build/<binary>` mtime-based rebuild logic in
`install.sh` is a silent-fail surface. Any committed
build artifact should have a content-integrity check
(strings-grep, byte-pattern, ELF-magic — whatever is
cheap and specific) rather than relying on mtime
comparisons against source. v6.0.0 closeout should
generalize the .45 contamination gate to cover every
committed `build/<binary>` artifact, not just cc5.


[[entries]]
name = "gvar_init_order_kmode_static_v51164"
description = "kmode==1 emit order puts gvar-init AFTER PARSE_PROG which never returns. Top-level `var X = INT_LITERAL ;` declarations got their runtime store CODE-EMITTED but never EXECUTED. agnos's XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS = 10000000 read as 0. v5.11.64 fixed via static-init tables (gvar_initval + gvar_byte_off) baking literal values into file image at FIXUP-time."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "kmode", "gvar", "agnos", "kernel", "static-init", "fixup", "v5.11.64"]
content = """
== SYMPTOM ==

agnos kernel main body reads a top-level gvar as 0 even
though source declares `var XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS =
10000000;`. Helper fn `_xhci_wait_command()` consumes the
value as a spin-loop iteration count; gets 0; loops never
execute; xHCI commands time out. 10+ iron-burn debug
attempts before the compiler-side root cause surfaced.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

In `kmode==1` mode (kernel target), cyrius emit order is:

  [boot-shim asm]
  → [rest of PARSE_PROG]
  → [EMIT_GVAR_INITS]   # the runtime stores

The boot-shim asm + PARSE_PROG body produce the kernel
mainline that NEVER RETURNS (kernel main is an infinite
event loop). EMIT_GVAR_INITS emits AFTER the mainline.
The code is in the binary but unreachable.

So the runtime store sequence `mov rax, 10000000; mov
[gvar_X_addr], rax` is generated correctly — but the CPU
never executes it. The gvar reads return the BSS-zero
default instead of the declared literal.

== WHY THIS DIDN'T BITE EARLIER ==

Pre-v5.11.x agnos consumers used compile-time constants
(enum variants, #define macros) for the kernel-mainline
parameters. The first agnos slot to declare a top-level
`var X = N;` for a magic number was the xHCI sub-arc —
which surfaced the bug.

Userland binaries don't hit this: `main()` RETURNS (to
exit syscall stub); EMIT_GVAR_INITS lands BEFORE main
in userland-emit order; gvars get initialized before
main runs.

== FIX (v5.11.64) ==

PARSE_GVAR_REG detects `= INT_LITERAL ;` shape with
positive non-zero RHS:

1. Records value in new `gvar_initval` table at
   0x1EC000 (8192 × 8B; uses freed `fn_ret_sid` slot
   relocated to 0x15A000 at v5.11.19).
2. SKIPS gvar_toks registration so EMIT_GVAR_INITS
   doesn't replay the runtime store.

FIXUP populates per-var byte offsets in stable
`gvar_byte_off` mirror at 0x1B0000 (8192 × 8B prefix-sum
mirror, FIXUP-time written / EMIT-time read).

Each EMIT_* path adds `_EMIT_GVAR_STATIC_INITS(S, O, bss_o)`
after its var-area zero-fill to S64 literal bytes at the
right offsets. Result: value is in the file image at first
read; no runtime store needed.

== CROSS-ARCH COVERAGE ==

Every EMIT_* that writes a var area to file:
- x86 EMITELF_KERNEL + EMITELF64_KERNEL + EMITELF_USER +
  EMITELF_SHARED + EMITELF_OBJ
- aarch64 EMITELF + EMITELF_KERNEL
- MachO x86_64 + ARM64 (ARM64 guarded by has_data == 1)
- PE EMITPE_EXEC (handles both Windows EXE + EFI Application)

cx bytecode opts OUT via `_TARGET_CX != 1` guard in
PARSE_GVAR_REG.

== SHADOW-DECLARATION OPT-OUT ==

When FINDVAR finds an existing match for the new var name
(shadow redecl), fall through to runtime-store. Caught
during bring-up by `math_constants.tcyr`: mabda's `var
F64_TWO = 0` shadowed by math's `var F64_TWO =
0x4000_0000_0000_0000` hit EMIT_GVAR_INITS's FINDVAR-last-
match semantics — mabda's runtime-store-of-zero targeted
math's address, clobbering the new static-init bytes.

== cc5 SIZE DELTA ==

cc5 SHRANK 875,336 → 872,952 B (-2,384 B) from eliminating
runtime stores for cc5's own TS_TOK_* literal gvars in
src/frontend/ts/. Static-init eliminates the per-gvar
`mov rax, imm64; mov [addr], rax` sequence (~11 bytes per
gvar) and replaces with file-image bytes (8 bytes per gvar
in the .data section). Net savings depends on gvar count
in the compiler's own source.

== LESSON ==

Any compiler-emit ordering that places initialization
AFTER an unreachable code path is a silent-fail. The fix
shape "bake value into file image at FIXUP-time" is the
right pattern for kmode-shaped consumers (kernels, EFI
apps, anything where main never returns). The pattern is
also CHEAPER (smaller binary) than runtime stores.

For agents debugging "gvar reads 0 in kernel mode":
1. `objdump -d` the binary; find the gvar's address.
2. Check whether EMIT_GVAR_INITS sequence appears BEFORE
   or AFTER kernel main's entry. If after, static-init
   wasn't applied — confirm `gvar_initval[]` was populated.
3. Common cause: shadow-redecl where FINDVAR returned a
   different gvar's slot. Check both source files for
   the same var name at module scope.
"""


---
**THE TRAP.** In `kmode==1` the emit order puts gvar-init AFTER `PARSE_PROG`,
which never returns. Top-level `var X = <int literal>;` declarations got their
runtime store **code-emitted but never executed** — so agnos's
`XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS = 10000000` read as **0**. A timeout of zero does not crash;
it makes a driver look like a hardware fault.

**FIX** (v5.11.64): static-init tables (`gvar_initval` + `gvar_byte_off`) bake the
literal values into the file image at FIXUP time, so no runtime store is needed.

**RECOGNIZE AGAIN**: emitted-but-unreachable initialisation. Code being present in
the binary is not evidence it runs — check the ORDER against any path that does not
return.

⟳ Full narrative in `retros/v511x.cyml`.
[[entries]]
name = "cve_05_tok_names_mangle_magic_budget_v51165"
description = "NPOS_GUARD(S, 256) was a magic-pessimistic budget that source-identifier byte-copy loops could SILENTLY exceed. tok_names region (256 KB @ 0x60000) could overflow into adjacent regions if source identifier + separator + dest identifier > 256 bytes. Worse: 4 of 6 main_*.cyr variants had NO tok_names guard at all on the use-alias mangle path. v5.11.65 replaced magic-256 with compute-source-length-then-NPOS_GUARD across 11 sites."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "tok_names", "mangle-path", "cve-05", "security", "cross-arch", "v5.11.65"]
content = """
== THE TRAP ==

`NPOS_GUARD(S, 256)` was a MAGIC-PESSIMISTIC BUDGET: the source-identifier
byte-copy loops could silently exceed it, overflowing the `tok_names` region
(256 KB @ 0x60000) into adjacent heap. Worse, 4 of 6 `main_*.cyr` forks had
NO guard at all on the use-alias mangle path.

FIX (v5.11.65): compute the source length, THEN NPOS_GUARD on the real
requirement — in every fork.

== RECOGNIZE AGAIN — two live classes ==

1. A guard whose bound is a ROUND NUMBER rather than a computed requirement is
   a guess, and a copy loop can exceed it silently.
2. A guard added to ONE per-target fork. `ls src/main*.cyr` is 7; any new check
   on a shared path must land in all of them, and only a cross-target gate
   catches the miss. Same shape as
   `new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx_backends...`.

Full narrative: retros/v511x.cyml. Compressed at the v6.6.1 gotchas audit —
this entry was 150 lines of backstory the retro already carried.
"""
[[entries]]
name = "macho_output_basename_breaks_reproducible_cmp"
description = "Mach-O embeds the output binary's basename (the ad-hoc code-signature identifier), so compiling the same source to two DIFFERENT names (fib vs fib2) yields binaries that differ by exactly the name bytes — a false 'non-reproducible'. ELF doesn't embed the name, so Linux passes and hides it. Corollary: never codesign-compare; codesign is non-deterministic."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "reproducibility", "codesign", "v6.0.63", "v6.0.44"]
content = """
**The trap**: a reproducible-build check compiled the same
source twice — to `build/fib` and `build/fib2` — then compared
hashes. They differed on macOS but matched on Linux. It looked
like macOS compiler non-determinism.

**Root cause**: the Mach-O embeds the OUTPUT BASENAME (it's the
ad-hoc code-signature identifier / a build id). Two different
output names → the binaries differ by exactly those name bytes.
`cmp -l` showed a SINGLE differing byte, at the offset of the
embedded name (`1` vs `2` in the "…fib1"/"…fib2" string). ELF
does NOT embed the output name, so the same check passed on
Linux and hid the issue for the whole platform.

**Confirm in 30s**: build the same source to the SAME output
name twice → byte-identical. Build to different names → differ
by the name. The compiler is deterministic; the test was wrong.

**Two rules for any Mach-O reproducibility / self-host check**:
1. Rebuild to the SAME output name, never name1/name2.
2. Hash/compare the UNSIGNED output, never the codesigned
   artifact — `codesign` is non-deterministic (the v6.0.44
   lesson: a codesign-compare produced a false "non-reproducible"
   retraction). To RUN a Mach-O on macOS you MUST codesign it
   (AMFI), so `cp` the unsigned bytes aside BEFORE signing and
   compare those. This bit the .63 functional gate AND the macho
   self-host check in the same session (a signed r1 vs unsigned
   r2 differs by ~20 KB of signature).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_test_apple_silicon_qemu_and_codesign"
description = "cyrius test/run/bench/fuzz/doctest failed on arm64 macOS via two stacked cbt bugs: (1) run_binary took the qemu-aarch64 cross-run branch on a NATIVE Apple-Silicon host (find_tools forces _arch=AARCH64) → no qemu → execve-fail → exit 127; (2) the compiled /tmp binary ran UNsigned → AMFI SIGKILL → exit 137. A compile-and-run command on macho must exec natively (never qemu) AND ad-hoc codesign first."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cbt", "macho", "apple-silicon", "codesign", "qemu", "run_binary", "cyrius-test", "v6.0.66"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius test` (and run/bench/fuzz/doctest) fail on
arm64 macOS with a bare **exit 127** and zero test output — only on
the macOS runner, unchanged by any compiler fix. Two cbt bugs stack:

**Bug 1 (the 127) — qemu mis-route.** `cbt/build.cyr run_binary`
had an `if (_arch == ARCH_AARCH64) { exec /usr/bin/qemu-aarch64 }`
branch — the x86-Linux-host path for cross-running aarch64 test
binaries under qemu-user. But `find_tools()` (cbt/core.cyr) forces
`_arch=AARCH64` on arm64-macOS so current_cc picks the native macho
cycc — which made run_binary ALSO take the qemu branch on a NATIVE
Apple-Silicon host, where there is no `/usr/bin/qemu-aarch64` →
execve fails → `sys_exit(127)`. The binary never runs (zero output;
the literal 127). Fix (v6.0.66): gate the qemu branch
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` — a native Mach-O host execs directly.

**Bug 2 (the next domino, 137) — unsigned AMFI SIGKILL.** Even with
Bug 1 fixed, the freshly-compiled `/tmp/cyrius_test_bin` is UNsigned,
and arm64 macOS AMFI SIGKILLs unsigned binaries (128+9=137). Only
`cmd_build` ad-hoc-codesigned (v6.0.38); test/run/bench/fuzz/doctest
compiled to /tmp and ran via run_binary without signing. Fix: hoist
the codesign into a shared `_macho_codesign()` and call it INSIDE
run_binary so every run path signs (cmd_build DRY'd to it). No-op off
macOS; harmless on x86-macOS (codesign -s - rejects but unsigned runs
there anyway).

**Why it masked on ecb but not the hosted runner**: ecb (the dev
Apple-Silicon box) had qemu-user installed, and qemu happily runs a
native-arch binary too (AMFI-bypassed) — so run_binary's qemu branch
"worked" there. The hosted macos-15-arm64 runner has neither qemu nor
(post-fix-1) a signature → it exposes both bugs. Classic ecb-vs-runner
split: "run it on the ACTUAL CI target, not just the dev box."

**Recognize again**: any cbt compile-and-run command exiting 127
(qemu/exec-fail, zero output) or 137 (AMFI SIGKILL) on Apple Silicon.
The rule: on macho, exec natively (never qemu) and `_macho_codesign`
the binary before exec.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_multi_dll_imports_byte_identical_refactor_and_cross_arch_stubs"
description = "v6.0.69: generalized the PE backend from ONE hardcoded kernel32 import descriptor to N DLLs (shell32, then dxgi). Three reusable lessons: (1) the flat ftype=4 IAT fixup assumed imp_idx==IAT-slot, so grouping imports by DLL into per-descriptor null-terminated IAT/ILT runs forced an imp_idx→physical-slot remap (_pe_iat_slot); (2) design the generalization to be BYTE-IDENTICAL at one DLL so it's a provable no-op before adding the 2nd; (3) PE-reroute emit fns live in x86/emit.cyr but parse_expr.cyr calls them inside a runtime `if (_TARGET_PE==1)` block that compiles on EVERY backend → aarch64/emit.cyr needs no-op stubs or it's FATAL under --strict."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pe", "windows", "imports", "iat", "ffi", "shell32", "byte-identical", "cross-arch-stub", "utf16", "v6.0.69"]
content = """
**Generalizing the PE import table from 1 DLL to N (the Windows FFI
foundation).** Pre-v6.0.69 the PE backend hardcoded a single kernel32
import descriptor: `_pe_imp_dir_size = 20*2` (one descriptor + null),
literal `"kernel32.dll"`, and a FLAT IAT/ILT where imp_idx directly
indexed the thunk slot. shell32 (CommandLineToArgvW) + dxgi
(CreateDXGIFactory1) both need a non-kernel32 descriptor.

**(1) The flat ftype=4 fixup is the constraint.** `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr`
resolves a PE IAT call as `iat_rva = idata + iat_off + imp_idx*8` — imp_idx
IS the physical slot. But a multi-DLL IAT must group each DLL's thunks into
a contiguous, per-descriptor null-terminated run (the loader walks each
descriptor's ILT to a null and fills the matching IAT run). Grouping breaks
imp_idx==slot. Fix: tag each import with a DLL id (`_pe_imp_dll`), in
`_pe_layout` walk DLL ids ascending (kernel32=0 FIRST so ExitProcess stays
at slot 0), assign physical slots per group with a null after each, and
record `_pe_iat_pos[imp_idx]` → consumed by the fixup via `_pe_iat_slot(idx)`.
The fixup FORMAT is unchanged (still a flat imp_idx); only the resolution
gains a lookup.

**(2) Byte-identical-at-N=1 is the safety proof.** Design every size/offset
so one DLL reproduces the old layout exactly: `(dll_count+1)*20` == `20*2`,
grouped order == imp_idx order, `_pe_iat_pos[i]==i`, one trailing null. Then
VERIFY before adding DLL #2: compile the same Windows program with the old
and new compiler → identical bytes (got an identical 2560-byte PE). A PE-
layout refactor that's a proven no-op at N=1 can't have regressed the 25
existing kernel32 imports; only the genuinely-new 2nd-DLL path is untested,
and the cass run covers that. This pattern (prove the generalization is
inert on the existing case, THEN extend) beats trying to eyeball RVA math.

**(3) Cross-arch stubs for x86-only emit fns.** The PE-reroute emitters
(`ECMDTOARGV_PE`, `ELOCALFREE_PE`, `ESLEEP_PE`, …) live in
`src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`. `src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr` calls them inside
a RUNTIME `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)` block (not `#ifdef`), so that code COMPILES
on the aarch64/macho/cx backends too — where the symbols don't exist. Each
needs a `fn EXXX_PE(S) { return 0; }` no-op stub in
`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` (the dead `_TARGET_PE` branch never runs
there). WITHOUT the stub it's a warning on a normal build but **FATAL under
--strict** — and cross-os pi surfaces the `undefined function` warning
loud. When you add ANY new PE-reroute fn, add its aarch64 stub in the SAME
slot (cross-arch-propagation-mandatory). cx has no stubs and tolerates the
warning (mirrors ESLEEP_PE) — match whatever the existing cohort does.

**Bonus — Windows argv is UTF-16.** GetCommandLineW / CommandLineToArgvW
return UTF-16LE. The v6.0.54 tokenizer down-converted ASCII-only (drop the
high byte) → non-ASCII paths corrupted silently. `_args_w2u8` (lib/args.cyr,
host-testable) does real UTF-16→UTF-8 incl. surrogate-pair decode (high
0xD800-0xDBFF + low 0xDC00-0xDFFF → one U+10000+ code point → 4-byte UTF-8).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_linux_esysxlat_gaps_native_divergence_placebo_gate"
description = "v6.0.68: the aarch64-Linux ESYSXLAT (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr, non-macho branch) was missing x86→aarch64 translations for literal syscalls cbt issues — stat 4 (ran as io_getevents → _file_size=-1 → `cyrius deps` include-scan short-circuited → transitive stdlib silently dropped = §D1), rename 82 (=fsync → `cyrius build` never renamed its .tmp output), symlink 88 (pulsar). PLUS: main_aarch64_native.cyr (the SHIPPED native cycc) silently diverged from main_aarch64.cyr — never got the v5.9.37 auto-call-main port → bare `fn main()` builds exited 0. PLUS: the CI gate tested the cross binary, not the shipped native one (placebo)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "esysxlat", "syscall", "deps", "rename", "symlink", "stat", "auto-call-main", "native-vs-cross", "placebo-gate", "found-by-ports", "v6.0.68"]
content = """
**Three bugs, one goal (make the aarch64-native funcgate HARD), all in
the aarch64-Linux NATIVE toolchain. Found because §D1's deps fix let the
soft funcgate finally reach its later steps — a classic mask-chain.**

**(1) ESYSXLAT is x86-numbered everywhere; a literal `syscall(N)` in cbt
needs an aarch64 translation or it hits the WRONG aarch64 syscall.**
The aarch64 backend translates x86 syscall numbers → aarch64 via the
ESYSXLAT cmp/b.ne/movz chain (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`, non-macho
branch, after the `_TARGET_MACHO==2` return). It was INCOMPLETE relative
to the macho branch. `cbt` issues several syscalls as bare literals:
- `stat 4`  → aarch64 4 is `io_getevents`. `_file_size` (cbt/deps.cyr)
  → -1 → the deps transitive-include scan does `if (sz<=0) return 0`
  → peers (`syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr`, `alloc_*`, `atomic`) silently
  dropped. THIS is §D1 ("cyrius deps silently fails on aarch64").
- `rename 82` → aarch64 82 is `fsync`. `cyrius build` builds to
  `out.tmp.<pid>` then renames; rename no-op'd → the final binary never
  appeared (rc=0, no output, no error).
- `symlink 88` → aarch64 has no symlink(2). `cyrius pulsar` install
  symlinks failed.
Fix: add `stat 4→fstatat 79`, `rename 82→renameat 38`, `symlink
88→symlinkat 36`, each with the AT_FDCWD arg-shift (mirror the existing
`openat 2→56` reroute). **Ordering gotcha:** put `stat 4→79` AFTER
`getcwd 79→17`, else the `x8=79` it sets is re-caught by `cmp x8,#79`
and double-translated to getcwd. (`fcntl 72` is macho-only F_GETPATH;
aarch64-Linux uses the getcwd `#else` path, so it needs no entry.)
Assembler-verify every hand-rolled encoding (`as` + `objdump -d` on the
target, `cmp` the bytes) — the renameat/symlinkat arg-shifts are easy to
get subtly wrong.

**(2) main_aarch64_native.cyr can silently diverge from
main_aarch64.cyr.** There are TWO aarch64 entry files — the CROSS
(`main_aarch64.cyr`, x86-host) and the NATIVE (`main_aarch64_native.cyr`,
aarch64-host → the SHIPPED `build/cycc-native-aarch64`). They share the
backend but each carries its OWN copy of the entry/epilogue logic,
including the v5.9.37 auto-call-`main` trampoline (walks the fn table for
"main\\0", emits ECALLTO). The cross had it; macho got it v6.0.37, win
v6.0.54 — the NATIVE never did. So `cycc-native-aarch64` never invoked a
bare `fn main()` → every such `cyrius build` on real ARM exited with junk
(0). It still SELF-HOSTS byte-identical because the compiler's own source
`sys_exit`s explicitly (no bare `fn main`). **When you port an
entry-level emit fix, grep ALL `main_*.cyr` — the per-target forks rot
independently, and the bug shows on USER programs, never on self-host.**

**(3) A gate that tests the wrong binary is a placebo.** The CI
`aarch64-native` job built/self-hosted/funcgated `cycc_a64` =
`main_aarch64.cyr` piped through the cross — NOT the shipped
`main_aarch64_native.cyr` binary. So bug (2) stayed green for the gate's
whole life (the cross had auto-call-main). Same disease as the macOS-rot
incident: **the gate must build + run the artifact users INSTALL** (here:
`main_aarch64_native.cyr → cycc_native_a64`, self-hosted from its OWN
source). Test what ships, not a same-arch lookalike.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "param_load_pseudo_and_bootstrap_coupling"
description = "asm-block param_load(reg, idx) (v6.0.67) emits a param load at the prologue-correct disp = -(idx+1+_cur_fn_regalloc)*8 — byte-identical to the hand-rolled [rbp-N]/[x29,#-N] literals (disp8 mov / ldur). BUT: migrating a BOOTSTRAP-CRITICAL lib (atomic.cyr is in cycc via the .64 alloc lock) to a NEW compiler feature couples that lib to the feature — a pre-feature cycc can't compile it (the smoke gate catches a stale install)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["asm", "param_load", "prologue", "bootstrap", "atomic", "regalloc", "v6.0.67"]
content = """
**The feature**: `param_load(reg, idx)` inside an `asm { }` block
emits a load of the idx-th parameter from its ACTUAL prologue slot,
so inline asm stops hardcoding `[rbp-N]` / `[x29,#-N]` byte literals
that silently break when the prologue layout shifts (the recurring
sigil AES-NI / cyrius atomic.cyr coupling bug). Implementation
(src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/emit.cyr): match "param_load" in
ASM_MNEMONIC (first-8 L64 `0x6F6C5F6D61726170` + verify trailing
"ad"), parse `(reg, idx)`, map reg name → number
(`_asm_{x86,aarch64}_reg`), emit at disp = -(idx+1+_cur_fn_regalloc)*8.
x86 → `48 8B (0x45|rl<<3) disp8` (mod=01, rm=rbp); aarch64 →
`0xF8400000 | ((disp&0x1FF)<<12) | (29<<5) | reg` (ldur). Tokens:
LPAREN=10, RPAREN=11, COMMA=31 (NOT 28 — that's LBRACKET).

**Byte-identical check**: param_load with regalloc=0 emits EXACTLY
the old hand-rolled literals (`param_load(rdi,0)` → `48 8B 7D F8`;
`param_load(x0,0)` → `A0 83 5F F8`). Migrating atomic.cyr produced a
byte-identical cycc — the cleanest possible correctness proof (atomic.cyr
is compiled INTO cycc via the v6.0.64 alloc lock).

**The bootstrap-coupling gotcha (the real lesson)**: migrating a
BOOTSTRAP-CRITICAL lib to a NEW compiler feature couples that lib to
the feature. atomic.cyr/thread.cyr are in cycc's own source → after
migration they REQUIRE a param_load-capable (≥6.0.67) cycc. A stale
installed cycc (or a frozen pre-feature stage) can't compile them —
the `cyrius smoke` gate (which uses the INSTALLED cycc on a .smcyr that
includes atomic.cyr) caught exactly this with a 6.0.66 install. Safe
because: consumers get lib+cycc together via `cyrius deps`; the bootstrap
propagates param_load from the CURRENT backend (cybs does NOT compile
main.cyr directly — `cat main.cyr | cybs` fails pre-existing, so cybs is
not the main.cyr compiler); pulsar's self-host chain carries it forward.
Smoke passes once .67 is installed. **Before migrating any bootstrap-
critical lib to a new feature, weigh this coupling** — for a consumer-
only feature you can ship it WITHOUT migrating cyrius's own libs.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "windows_install_pillar_env_spawn_exe"
description = "Making `cyrius build` work on Windows took three wrapper layers, each invisible until run on real cass: (1) no /proc env-read on Windows; (2) the wrapper looked for `cycc` not `cycc.exe` and defaulted _home=/root; (3) compile()/run_binary use POSIX fork/dup2/execve which Win32 lacks."
detail = """
v6.0.85. The cross-OS gate had cycc.exe SELF-HOSTING green for a long time, but
`cyrius` (the wrapper) could neither find nor run it on Windows - so a real
install was unusable. Each layer surfaced only by running on cass (do NOT trust
the self-host verdict for the install pillar; see the methodology notes).

1. ENV READ. cbt/core.cyr + lib/io.cyr + _read_env all read /proc/self/environ
   (Linux) or _macho_fill_environ (macOS). Windows has NEITHER, so CYRIUS_HOME /
   USERPROFILE were invisible and _home defaulted to "/root". Fix: a new PE
   reroute syscall(0xF015) -> kernel32!GetEnvironmentVariableA (3 args:
   name->rcx, buf->rdx, size->r8; needs a 3-arg 16-byte-aligned caller
   _pe_call_3arg_aligned). Register the import in pe/emit.cyr
   (_pe_register_kernel32), emit in x86/emit.cyr, dispatch in parse_expr.cyr,
   aarch64 no-op stub (FATAL under --strict otherwise).

2. .EXE SUFFIX. current_cc() (cbt/core.cyr) resolved <home>/bin/cycc. Windows
   binaries are cycc.exe - guard the name with #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN.

3. SPAWN. compile() (cbt/build.cyr) and run_binary() invoke cycc via sys_fork +
   sys_dup2 + sys_execve - all POSIX, absent on Win32. Also SYS_RENAME(82) and
   getpid(39) are NOT PE reroutes, so the atomic tmp+rename block can't run on
   Windows. Fix: a #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN branch that spawns
   `cycc < source > output` via cmd.exe + CreateProcessW (lib/process_win.cyr
   _win_compile_spawn), writing directly to `output`. cmd does the redirect.
   The quoting that works: `cmd /s /c "<inner>"` - /s strips ONLY the outermost
   quote pair, so per-path quotes + the < > operators reach cycc intact:
   cmd /s /c ""C:\...\cycc.exe" < "src.cyr" > "out.exe""

4. .ps1 ASCII. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads scripts as the system ANSI
   codepage, so a UTF-8 em-dash (or any multi-byte char) breaks the parser
   ("Unexpected token"). Keep install.ps1 / cass-install-gate.ps1 ASCII-only.

5. deps --lock hash: no /bin/sh or sha256sum on Windows; use
   `certutil -hashfile <p> SHA256` and parse line 2 (the 64-hex digest;
   certutil prints a header line first, \r\n endings). cbt/deps.cyr.

The install pillar is gated by scripts/cass-install-gate.{sh,ps1} (cyrius audit):
real install.ps1 -> cyrius build fn main(){return 42} -> assert exit 42 - the
Windows analog of the ecb install gate. The release tarball (build-windows-
tarball.sh, single source of truth) now ships cyrius.exe + install.ps1; it
previously shipped neither, so even a "successful" download had no wrapper.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "x86_macho_byte_array_literal_self_host_invisible"
description = "The x86-macOS (Mach-O) cycc cannot compile byte-array literals (`var foo[N] = { ... };`) — a parser miscompile that is INVISIBLE to self-host because cycc's own source uses no byte-array literals, so the PARSE_GVAR_ARR path is never exercised when cycc compiles itself. The target self-hosts byte-identical while carrying a latent miscompile of a path it never runs on itself."
detail = """
v6.0.88 (surfaced by the cross-OS lib-test on ach when byte_array_literal.tcyr was
the glob). On ach the native Mach-O cycc aborts compiling the test with
`error:2358: expected ';', got '='` (line is inside the prepended stdlib includes,
not the test's byte-array lines → general parse-state corruption, x86-Mach-O only).
A trivial `syscall(60,42)` compiles+runs fine, so Mach-O execution is healthy — the
break is at parse/compile time on this build.

WHY SELF-HOST NEVER CAUGHT IT: cycc has zero `var x[N] = {...}` literals, so
PARSE_GVAR_ARR is dead during cycc's own compile. The x86-Mach-O cycc self-hosts
byte-identical (green every slot) yet miscompiles a parser path it never self-uses.
Exact "green checkmark over untested codegen" class — cf. the macOS-rot incident
(macho_syscall_number_dual_definition…) and feedback_macos_windows_ci_gate_mandatory.

VERIFY-IT-IS-PRE-EXISTING TRICK: stash the working tree to the released tag's source
on the target and rebuild the native cycc there — the released .87 fails identically,
proving a given slot's emit-layer change didn't cause a *parse* error (a codegen
change cannot). Gated behind the x86-macOS-self-compile HELD decision (Intel/EOL);
filed issues/2026-06-07-x86-macho-byte-array-literal-no-compile.md.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "byte_array_peephole_obj_aware_evrcx_not_evaddr_x1"
description = "When hoisting `&var` once for the byte-array-literal peephole, use the OBJ-AWARE base loader (_EVRCX: rip-relative LEA when _IS_OBJ, else absolute mov), NOT EVADDR_X1 (absolute-mov only). EVADDR_X1 would silently regress byte arrays compiled to relocatable objects (`-c`) / PIE on x86. aarch64/cx alias _EVRCX to their already-correct EVADDR_X1 (aarch64's has the Mach-O adrp/add branch)."
detail = """
v6.0.88. The peephole replaces the ~7-op per-byte EVADDR/EADDRA_IMM/PUSH/MOVI/POPC/
STORE8/XORAA chain with: hoist base into rcx/x1/r1 ONCE + one direct store per byte
(x86 `mov byte [rcx+disp],imm8` C6/0 disp8|disp32; aarch64 `strb w0,[x1,#imm12]`
with a >=4096 legacy fallback; cx `store8 [r1(+disp)],r0`). ~5x emit compression +
one fixup per array.

THE TRAP: the obvious "load &var into rcx" primitive EVADDR_X1 emits `mov rcx,imm64`
unconditionally — fine for a non-PIE executable, WRONG for an object/PIE target that
needs rip-relative + a relocation. The old per-byte code used EVADDR (rax) which HAS
the _IS_OBJ rip-relative branch; the naive peephole port to EVADDR_X1 drops it.
_EVRCX is the rcx-twin of EVADDR's obj-aware logic — already present in x86 emit.cyr,
used by struct-global field stores. cycc has no byte arrays → self-host byte-identical
regardless; the bug only shows in obj-mode CONSUMER codegen.

IR mode (CYRIUS_IR=1|2, opt-in) keeps the legacy IR-aware per-byte sequence so the
IR stream is unchanged; the peephole is the IR-off (default / every real build) path.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "ts_children_list_reserve_then_fill_corrupts"
description = "TS parser children-list building: NEVER `off = TS_AST_CHILDREN_RESERVE(...)` then parse the elements into `off+i`. RESERVE does not advance the pool cursor, so each element's own sub-lists allocate at the SAME offset and stomp the parent (and vice versa) — a corrupt AST for any nested list. Use the deferred path: `base = TS_CST_TOP()`; parse each element and `TS_CST_PUSH(idx)` AFTER it's fully parsed; then `off = TS_CST_FLUSH(ts_base, base)`. The corruption was invisible for years because --parse-ts never reads lists back. v6.1.10."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "parser", "children-list", "ast", "TS_CST_PUSH", "TS_CST_FLUSH", "v6.1.10"]
content = """
**The trap**: every `src/frontend/ts/parse.cyr` list builder used
to do `off = TS_AST_CHILDREN_RESERVE(ts_base, N)` and then parse
its elements, writing each to `off + i`. But RESERVE only *peeks*
the pool cursor — it does NOT advance `used` (only COMMIT does). So
when an element is parsed, that element's own sub-lists allocate at
the SAME `used` (= off) and overwrite the parent's slots, and the
parent's later SETs overwrite the child's. Any nested list (call
args, block stmts, object props, JSX attrs/children, var bindings,
switch cases, ...) came out garbage.

**Why you won't see it in a quick check**: `cycc --parse-ts <f>`
exits 0 — but parse "success" only means no parse ERROR fired. It
never walks the retained AST, so the corruption is invisible until
something reads the lists. For years nothing did.

**The fix / the rule** (v6.1.10): deferred construction. Builders
push fully-parsed element indices onto the `_ts_cst` scratch stack
and flush one contiguous block at the end:

    var base = TS_CST_TOP();
    while (...) { var e = parse_element(); ...; TS_CST_PUSH(e); }
    var off = TS_CST_FLUSH(ts_base, base);   # contiguous, advances cursor
    if (off < 0) { return 0 - 1; }

An element is pushed only AFTER its subtree is parsed, so a run
stays contiguous on the stack even when nested builders push/flush
in between (each saves TS_CST_TOP() and flushes back to it). Two
interleaved lists at once (the template quasi/expr pair) collect
into locals + `TS_AST_CHILDREN_WRITE`. Only VALUE-walked builders
need this; type-only builders can stay on the old path (they can't
corrupt a value list once value FLUSHes advance the cursor past
them). Verify with `cycc --emit-js` (self-validates: non-zero on
any node revisited / out-of-range). Full backstory:
retros/v61x.cyml (v61x_ts_ast_allocator_corruption).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "ts_trailing_comma_generic_params_tsx"
description = "TS_PARSE_TYPE_PARAMS must accept a trailing comma before `>`: the TSX disambiguation form `const f = async <T,>(x) => x` writes `<T,>` (the comma stops the parser reading `<T>` as a JSX open tag). Pre-v6.1.10 the param loop demanded another IDENT after a comma, so every `.tsx` generic arrow failed to parse — including the consumer's real app.tsx. After consuming the comma, re-check for a `>`-family closer and stop. v6.1.10."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "parser", "tsx", "generics", "type-params", "v6.1.10"]
content = """
**The trap**: in `.tsx`, a bare `<T>(x) => x` is ambiguous with a
JSX element, so TypeScript's disambiguation is the trailing comma:
`<T,>(x) => x`. Cyrius's `TS_PARSE_TYPE_PARAMS` consumed the comma
and then unconditionally expected another type-param IDENT, so it
errored on the `>` — every generic arrow in a `.tsx` file failed
to parse, which is why the consumer's real `web/app.tsx` wouldn't
parse at all (`async <T,>(...)` twice).

**The fix** (v6.1.10): after `TS_PARSE_EXPECT(COMMA)` in the
param loop, peek for a closing token (`TS_TOK_GT` / `GT_GT` /
`GT_GT_GT` / `GT_EQ` / ...). If found, it's a trailing comma —
set done and stop instead of demanding another param. `<T>`
(no comma) already worked; this just makes `<T,>` work too.

Recognize it: a `.tsx` consumer reports "parses fine in tsc but
cyrius --parse-ts errors near a generic arrow." The bare `.ts`
equivalent (no JSX ambiguity, written `<T>`) parses, so it looks
JSX-specific — it's the trailing comma, not the JSX.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_server_socket_accept_commits_v6222"
description = "AGNOS gained inbound TCP at kernel 1.45.5/.6 (sock_listen#56 / sock_accept#57); cyrius v6.2.22 wires lib/net.cyr's sock_bind/listen/accept via a listen<->fd adapter. The non-obvious trap: sock_accept(#57) COMMITS the accept kernel-side (the conn is ESTABLISHED + marked accepted, never re-handed-out), so if the LOCAL 8-slot fd table is full you MUST reap the returned conn_id (#50) or it leaks a kernel slot forever. Also: a consumer's 'undefined CLONE_VM on --agnos' is almost always STALE vendored thread.cyr, not a lib bug."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["agnos", "sockets", "net.cyr", "syscall", "v6.2.22", "thread.cyr", "stale-vendored"]
content = """
**AGNOS server sockets (v6.2.22).** The kernel exposes inbound TCP as two
1-arg syscalls: `sock_listen(port)#56` (merges bind+listen -> a listen_id
0..7) and `sock_accept(listen_id)#57` (non-blocking -> next ESTABLISHED
conn_id 0..7, or -1 = WOULD_BLOCK). `lib/net.cyr`'s `sock_bind`/`sock_listen`/
`sock_accept` route onto these on `--agnos` via a **listen<->fd adapter** in
`lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr` (parallel to the v6.2.3 conn<->fd adapter):
bind stashes the port, listen issues #56, accept issues #57 + wraps the
conn_id in a fresh tagged fd riding the existing #48/#49/#50 path. Both are
1-arg, so NO codegen change.

**Trap 1 — #57 COMMITS the accept.** Unlike a peek, `tcp_accept` sets the
kernel conn's accepted-bit and never re-hands it out (and `tcp_close` on the
listener skips accepted children). So in `sock_accept`, after #57 returns a
conn_id, if your local fd allocation fails (the cyrius 8-slot table is full:
1 listen + 7 conns), you MUST `sys_sock_close(cid)` (#50) BEFORE returning the
error — otherwise that kernel conn slot leaks forever and, over the server's
life, the kernel's 8-slot table is exhausted. (The client #47 path has no such
window — the fd is allocated BEFORE the kernel call.) Caught by adversarial
review, not the compile-only gate.

**Trap 2 — clear ALL slot tables on close.** A bind-without-listen (or bind
then a FAILED listen) close must zero `listen_port` too, or a recycled fd-slot
inherits a stale port and silently mis-listens. `sys_close` clears conn_tbl +
listen_tbl + listen_port uniformly on every socket-fd close.

**Trap 3 — 'undefined CLONE_VM on --agnos' is NOT a lib bug.** `lib/thread.cyr`
self-guards agnos since v6.2.3 (`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` -> thread_agnos.cyr
no-op mutexes; the `mmap.cyr`/CLONE_VM body is `#ifndef`-gated). A stdlib
consumer hitting `undefined variable 'CLONE_VM'` on `--agnos` is almost always
running a STALE vendored `thread.cyr` (pre-v6.2.3) — the fix is `cyrius deps`
to refresh, NOT a compiler/lib change. (This is why sankoch 2.4.4's lock no-op
is self-sufficiency, not a crash fix — CHANGELOG 6.2.22.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_sysxlat_imm8_signext_getdents_v6234"
description = "EMACHO_SYSXLAT's _msx helper emits `cmp rax, imm8` (48 83 F8 ib), which SIGN-EXTENDS the byte. Any Linux syscall number 128..255 routed through _msx silently never matches (217=0xD9 -> -39), so the BSD translation is skipped and the raw Linux number reaches the Darwin `syscall` -> SIGSYS. getdents64(217) hit this on x86-macOS; use _msx32 (imm32 cmp) for any number > 127. Self-host-blind (cycc never lists a dir) — found by ports. v6.2.34."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "x86", "EMACHO_SYSXLAT", "syscall", "imm8", "sign-extend", "getdents", "self-host-blind", "v6.2.34"]
content = """
**x86-macOS syscall translation: `_msx` imm8 sign-extension (v6.2.34).**
`EMACHO_SYSXLAT` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr) rewrites each Linux syscall number
in `rax` to its Darwin BSD number at emit time, one `_msx(lin, classed)` entry
per syscall. `_msx` hand-rolls `cmp rax, lin` as `48 83 F8 ib` — the `83 /7 ib`
form, whose immediate is an **8-bit value sign-extended to 64 bits**. So for any
`lin` in 128..255 the compare is against a NEGATIVE number: `getdents64` 217 =
`0xD9` sign-extends to `-39`, `cmp rax, -39` never matches `rax = 217`, the
`mov rax, 0x2000158` (getdirentries64 344) is skipped, and the **raw Linux 217**
reaches the Darwin `syscall` instruction -> `SIGSYS` (exit 140) on every
directory listing.

**Rule:** route any syscall number > 127 through `_msx32` (`48 3D id` =
`cmp rax, imm32`), exactly as getrandom 318 already did. `_msx` is safe ONLY for
lin <= 127. 217 was the lone offender in the 128..255 gap.

**Why it survived for ~minors:** purely **self-host-blind** — cycc reads its
sources by path and never walks a directory, so the self-host gate (even on
real macOS) cannot exercise getdents64. The aarch64 backend translated 217
correctly all along (wide-immediate `cmp x8,#217`), so it was an x86-only gap.
Caught by a fresh consumer install + a backend imm-width review; PROVEN on real
x86 Darwin (`ach`): pre-fix binary SIGSYS (exit 140), post-fix lists the dir
(exit 3). The textbook "found by ports" / "self-host is a placebo for dir ops"
failure class. Closes live half of 2026-06-02-macos-x86-release-no-compiler.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "forward_call_prescan_and_monomorph_substrate"
description = "v6.3.5 revived two generics prerequisites and exposed three non-obvious facts about cycc's driver structure. (1) The driver is 3-PASS: pass-1 registers structs/enums/globals (and now fn signatures), pass-2 emits fn BODIES (PARSE_FN_DEF), pass-3 (PARSE_PROG) emits top-level statements. So the CO-01 'forward call gets mask 0' bug only bites a call INSIDE a fn body (pass-2) to a fn defined LATER — a top-level call always sees full masks because pass-2 finished all defs before pass-3 runs. A test with a top-level forward call proves nothing. (2) Pass-1 STOPS at the first top-level statement (catch-all `scan = 0` in main.cyr's pass-1 loop), so the prescan — like struct/global registration — only reaches declarations BEFORE the first statement. The standard decls-before-statements (C-style: fns first, `main()`/asserts last) order is fully covered; a fn defined after `alloc_init()` is NOT prescanned. (3) The inline-replay EMIT path (PARSE_FNCALL, the `GFINL != 0` branch) was never removed — only the body-token CAPTURE was gated off (`_INLINE_OK = 0` per backend). Re-enabling capture (v6.3.5's opt-in `_MONOMORPH_OK` / `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1`) revives value-inlining with no path rewrite."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["prescan", "forward-call", "abi", "3-pass", "fn-table", "monomorphization", "inline-replay", "co-01", "ar-01", "v6.3.5"]
content = """
**CO-01 — forward-call ABI prescan.** A call lexically before its callee's definition used to
auto-register the callee with mask 0 (PARSE_FNCALL's `if (fi < 0) { fi = REGFN }`), silently dropping
`: Str` auto-coercion, SIMD-arg routing, and the struct-return retptr ABI. Fix: a pass-1
`_prescan_fn_sig` (wired into all 7 forks' pass-1 in place of the inline fn-skip) REGFNs every
top-level fn and records its masks/count/variadic/return-type before pass-2 emits any call. Shared
`_classify_param_type` / `_classify_return_type` (extracted from PARSE_FN_DEF, differential-verified
byte-identical) keep prescan and definition in lockstep.

**The cursor-reset trick.** `_prescan_fn_sig` extracts metadata on a THROWAWAY cursor (advancing
through the signature however the scan needs), then `STI(S, start_ti)` and replays the verbatim
pre-CO-01 skip (`_prescan_skip_fn`). So pass-1's NET token-cursor advance is byte-identical to before —
the only new effect is the registration + mask writes (state, not cursor). This is why the prescan
doesn't perturb pass-1's downstream parsing.

**The `)` gotcha (cost hours).** `_prescan_params` originally returned with the cursor still on `)`
(it never consumed the closing paren), so the return-type block in `_prescan_fn_sig` saw `)` instead
of `:` and NEVER classified `: Struct` returns → `SFRS` stayed 0 → a forward call to a struct-returning
fn kept the scalar-return ABI → segfault. Fix: `if (PEEKT(S) == 11) { STI(S, GTI(S) + 1); }` at the end
of `_prescan_params`. Lesson: when a helper is followed by a sibling scan, it must leave the cursor
where the sibling expects it.

**How to debug 'the prescan is a no-op'.** Symptom: forward call still wrong, prescan-on == prescan-off
binary. First check whether the fn was even REACHED by pass-1 (instrument the prescan, or print GFNC at
pass-2 start) — if the callee is defined after a top-level statement, pass-1 stopped before it (fact 2
above). Then check the CALL site resolves the prescanned fi (FINDFN must find it) vs re-REGFNing a
fresh mask-0 slot.

**AR-01 — monomorph substrate.** `_MONOMORPH_OK` (util.cyr) ORs into PARSE_FN_DEF's two capture gates.
**As of v6.4.0 the default is 1 (util.cyr:273) — generics are DEFAULT-ON; `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` opts
out** (this was `default 0` / `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1`-opt-in when this entry was first written at v6.3.5).
The default-on flip was NOT a one-liner: the capture arm was DECOUPLED from general inlining by
qualifying it with `GFTP(S, fi) != 0` (only generic fns are captured; see
`monomorph_default_on_flip_gftp_decouple`) so the flip doesn't re-enable value-inlining for every fn.
The per-backend `_INLINE_OK = 0` default is untouched — x86 was disabled for code SIZE at v1.11.3,
aarch64 for a metadata overlap since relocated to S+0x9EA000. When the gate fires the intact replay
emits per-call-site value-substituted code. `_monomorph_substrate_gate` was **repurposed** at the flip:
it originally proved the substrate ACTIVE (compile a fixture ±flag → both exit 42, binaries differ) and
now proves the DECOUPLE holds (non-generic fns stay un-inlined under flag-on). TYPE substitution
(generics) builds on this in v6.3.7 — the substrate substitutes VALUES today, not types yet.

**Filed alongside:** `var p = mk()` (inferred local type from a struct-returning call) segfaults even
NON-forward — a pre-existing var-decl/retptr defect, not CO-01. Use `var p: T = mk()`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state"
description = "A cyrius closure literal `|params| body` is an anonymous fn (`__clNNNNN`) that PARSE_FACTOR emits INLINE inside the enclosing fn's code range (an EJMP0 jumps over it) while sharing the enclosing fn's per-fn codegen globals AND the per-fn local-slot tables. That sharing is the root of four v6.3.7 closure bugs: (1) the closure inherited `_cur_fn_regalloc` from an auto-regalloc'd enclosing fn, so ESTOREPARM shifted its param slots past phantom callee-saved saves and its locals were register-allocated to stale callee regs (`|x| x` returned 0); (2) the closure called ESUBRSP but never EPATCHFRAME, so its `sub rsp,imm32` kept the 0 placeholder — a 0-byte frame whose first body push clobbered param slot 0; (3) the closure reset SFLC(0) and wrote its params/locals into the SHARED name/depth/type/slice tables (S+0x5D9D000 / 0x5DBD000 / 0x5DDD000 / 0x5DFD000) starting at slot 0, overwriting the enclosing fn's locals at those slots (`var c0` shadowed by a later `|x|` became 'undefined variable'); (4) the enclosing fn's regalloc post-pass (the byte-scan at parse_fn.cyr `if (_cur_fn_regalloc > 0)`) scanned the INLINE closure bytes and NOP-patched/reallocated them → SIGILL when the closure was called."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closure", "lambda", "codegen", "regalloc", "inline-emit", "local-tables", "per-fn-state", "frame-patch", "v6.3.7"]
content = """
**A closure is a nested fn sharing the parent's mutable codegen state.** cyrius
has no general nested fns, so the closure path (`PARSE_FACTOR`, token 35 `|` or
54 `||`) is the only place a fn body is emitted WHILE another fn is mid-emit. It
reuses the parent's per-fn globals and per-fn slot tables, so it must save / reset
/ restore ALL of them — not just `in_fn` / `flc` / `sdep`.

**The four fixes (v6.3.7), all in `parse_expr.cyr`'s closure branch:**

1. **Per-fn codegen state.** Save + zero `_cur_fn_regalloc`, `_cur_fn_ret_stash`,
   `_cur_fn_ret_sid`, `_cur_fn_ret_scalar`, `_cur_fn_naked`, set `_cur_fn_ix` to the
   closure's fi; restore after. A closure takes no `#regalloc` / struct-ret /
   `#naked`. The regalloc leak was the param bug — disasm showed `mov [rbp-0x30],rdi`
   (param shifted by 5×8) and the body loading `a` from an unloaded `rbx`.

2. **Frame patch.** Capture `ESUBRSP`'s patch handle and `EPATCHFRAME(handle,
   (GFLC*8+15)&-16)` after the body, exactly like PARSE_FN_DEF. (The v6.2.41 change
   that dropped ESUBRSP's size arg claimed "patched later via EPATCHFRAME" but the
   closure path never called it — and there were no closure tests to catch it.)

3. **Local-table isolation.** Before SFLC(0), snapshot the enclosing range
   `[0, parent_flc)` of all four slot tables into an alloc'd buffer
   (`_cl_save_locals`); restore it after the closure (`_cl_restore_locals`).

4. **Skip the enclosing fn's regalloc post-pass.** A new global
   `_cur_fn_has_closure` is set in the closure branch and gates the post-pass:
   `if (_cur_fn_regalloc > 0 && _cur_fn_has_closure == 0)`. Skipping it keeps a
   correct, un-reallocated frame (the prologue's callee-saved saves are just
   unused) — the only cost is losing the optimization for that one fn. The clean
   long-term fix is to emit closures OUTSIDE the parent's byte range, but the skip
   is correct and minimal.

**Calling convention.** A closure value is a plain fn pointer (`ELOAD_FN_ADDR`,
same fixup type as `&fn`), so `callptr` / `fncallN` invoke it; `||` is a
zero-param closure at a factor position (binary `a || b` never reaches there as a
leading token). Names are `__clNNNNN` (5 zero-padded digits of GFNC) — the old
`__cl<clid%10>` single digit aliased the 11th closure onto the 1st in REGFN.

**LESSON:** any new "emit a fn body mid-emit" path must isolate the FULL per-fn
state (codegen globals + the 4 slot tables) AND stay clear of the parent's
byte-scan passes. A feature with zero tests (closures had none) hid all four for
many releases. `cycc` itself uses no closures, so self-host + the differential are
byte-identical — the only signal is a dedicated `closures.tcyr`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object"
description = "v6.3.8 added lexical capture by value to cyrius closures: a closure body that reads an enclosing local (a free var) captures it by value into a heap env object `[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`, and the closure VALUE becomes that object (not a bare fn pointer). It works by a PRE-SCAN of the closure body before emitting its prologue (`_cl_prescan_captures` in parse_expr.cyr): a name that FINDLOCAL-fails in the closure's own scope, is not a param/shadow, is not an `IDENT(`-shaped call name, and IS present in the enclosing-fn snapshot is recorded as a capture. If anything is captured, the closure gains a HIDDEN env param at arg-reg 0 / slot 0 and all user params shift to 1+; captured reads in the body become `load64(env + (1+capidx)*8)`. At construction the parent emits `alloc((1+ncap)*8)`, stores the fn addr at obj[0] and each captured value at obj[1+i], and the closure expression evaluates to the object. `callptr` auto-dispatches: if the callee local carries the `CLOSURE_TYID` (0x40000003) var type-tag it loads the fn ptr from `[obj]` and passes obj as the hidden arg1 (see closure semantics)."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["closure", "lambda", "lexical-capture", "by-value", "env-object", "callptr", "alloc", "var-type-tag", "v6.3.8"]
body = """
**Capture is by value into a vtable-shaped heap object.** A capturing closure
is no longer a bare fn pointer — it's `alloc((1+ncap)*8)` laid out as
`[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`. The closure fn gains a hidden env param (arg-reg 0 /
local slot 0); user params shift up by one (`ESTOREPARM(S, clpc + 1, …)` and the
fn's stored param count `_fnt_params[clfi]` is bumped). A captured read compiles
to `EFLLOAD(env) ; EADDRA_IMM((1+capidx)*8) ; ELOAD64`. The closure VALUE is the
object; a `var f = |x| base + x;` local is tagged `CLOSURE_TYID` so the call site
knows to dispatch it. Non-capturing closures are unchanged (still a bare fn ptr,
no allocation) — only a body that actually reads an enclosing local pays for an
env object.

**`callptr` auto-dispatch.** When the callee is a bare ident whose local type-tag
is `CLOSURE_TYID`, `callptr` spills the object, loads `[obj]` as the fn pointer,
pushes the object as a synthetic arg1, and calls indirect. Call sites are
identical to the non-capturing form — `callptr(f, 2)` works whether `f` captured
or not. This is the "auto-dispatch" calling convention chosen over a separate
`callclosure` builtin.

**THE non-obvious bug — stale name in the construction scratch slot.** The env
construction needs a scratch local slot to hold the freshly-alloc'd object while
it stores fn-addr + captures. That slot is a real entry in the shared local
name/depth/type/slice tables (`S+0x5D9D000` …), and those tables are NOT cleared
between fns — a slot can still hold a name string from a PREVIOUS fn. If the
scratch slot is left named, a later `var f` in this fn whose name happens to
collide trips the duplicate-variable check ("duplicate variable f") even though
the user only declared it once. Two same-name capturing closures in one fn hit
this every time. FIX: anonymize the scratch slot the instant it's claimed —
`S64(S + 0x5D9D000 + slot*8, 0 - 1); SLDEP(S, slot, 0);` (name = -1, depth 0).
The same anonymization is applied to `callptr`'s obj/fn-ptr spill slots.

**Platform.** PE (Windows) capturing closures SHIP as of v6.4.26 — routed
through `ECALLPTR_PE` (the 16-byte-aligned path) with the env/fn-pointer pushed
as the callee (parse_expr.cyr "v6.4.26: capturing closures now supported on PE");
the "capturing closures not yet supported on Windows PE" error string is gone
from src/. Capture is verified byte-correct on x86_64 + aarch64 (real pi + qemu)
and now on real cass.

**LESSON:** when you borrow a shared per-fn table slot for scratch, ANONYMIZE it
— the tables carry stale names across fns, and a live name in a scratch slot is
a landmine for the duplicate-decl check. Tests: `closures_capture.tcyr`
(single / multi / param-mix / by-value / thunk / with-locals / two-closures),
all via `callptr`. `cycc` uses no closures, so the only signal is the dedicated
tcyr — self-host + differential stay byte-identical.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash"
description = "v6.3.9 shipped i64 generic FUNCTIONS (`fn foo<T>(...)`) on the AR-01 token-replay substrate, gated behind _MONOMORPH_OK (default off → cycc byte-identical). The key architectural choice: in an i64-everywhere language the BASE fn is its own i64 instantiation — the body is emitted ONCE with `T -> i64` bound (via _bind_generic_call(S, fi, -8, -8) right before PARSE_PROG in PARSE_FN_DEF), so i64-typed calls are plain normal calls to the base, no instantiate-once machinery needed. `<T>` names are captured at the signature (_capture_tparams, replacing SKIP_GENERICS, gated) into _fnt_tparams (lazy-alloc, 0=non-generic); a scoped T->concrete binding (_tp_n0/_tp_c0/_tp_n1/_tp_c1) is consulted at the 4 type-resolution sites (param-type, local `: T`, slice<T>, sizeof) by _tp_resolve, with canonical token <0=-scalarwidth / >0=struct-sid. THE load-bearing bug: a `: T` generic return type made the return-type ROUGH-SCAN (the pre-param-loop lookahead that sets _cur_fn_ret_stash) default to stash=8 (struct retptr ABI), reserving arg-reg 0 for a hidden retptr and shifting every user param by one — so `id<T>(x:T):T{return x}` stored x into rsi's slot and read 0. Non-i64 instantiation + explicit `foo<i64>(x)` + generic structs are v6.3.10."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "type-param", "ret-stash", "retptr-abi", "i64-everywhere", "base-as-instance", "v6.3.9"]
body = """
**The base fn IS the i64 monomorphization.** Cyrius is i64-everywhere (ADR-002),
so v6.3.9 does NOT defer a generic fn's body or build instantiate-once for i64.
PARSE_FN_DEF binds every captured type-param to i64 (`_bind_generic_call(S, fi,
0-8, 0-8)`) immediately before `PARSE_PROG` emits the body, so the body emits as
the i64 instance. An i64-typed call (inferred or `id(42)`) is an ordinary normal
call to that base — no minting, no jump-over, no replay. Dedup is by construction
(one base = one i64 instance). Non-i64 type-args (`foo<i32>`, `foo<Point>`) need a
separate instance and are deferred to v6.3.10, where the closure-style
instantiate-once is shared with generic structs.

**THE gotcha — `: T` return ⇒ phantom retptr/stash ⇒ param shift.** A `: T`
generic return type segfaulted/returned-garbage for ANY body that read a param
(`return x` gave 0; `return a` gave arg1). Root cause: PARSE_FN_DEF runs a
RETURN-TYPE ROUGH-SCAN *before* the param loop (a lookahead past `()` to `: Type`)
that decides `_cur_fn_ret_stash` — and `_cur_fn_ret_stash > 0` makes the param
loop reserve arg-reg 0 (RDI/RCX) for a hidden struct retptr, shifting user param 0
into the SECOND arg reg. For `: T`, `FINDSTRUCT(T)` misses and `T` matches no
scalar name, so the rough-scan fell through to the default `_cur_fn_ret_stash = 8`
— treating the generic return as a big-struct retptr. Disasm of the broken `id`:
`mov rdi,-0x30 ; mov rsi,-0x38 ; … mov rax,-0x38` (TWO param stores for a 1-param
fn, body reads the second = rsi, which `id(42)` never sets → 0). Fix: in the
rough-scan, recognize a captured type-param (`_is_tparam_of(S, fi, _rs_rt_noff)`)
as i64-shaped (`_rs_is_scalar = 1`) so it does NOT set the stash. The *other*
return-type site (the post-param classify) already takes `_rtc = -8` for `: T` via
the same `_is_tparam_of` check — both sites must agree the generic return is
scalar.

**LESSON.** A new "abstract" type spelling (`T`) that isn't a struct and isn't a
known scalar will silently fall into whatever the type machinery's DEFAULT is — and
for return types that default is the retptr/big-struct ABI. Any place that
branches on "struct vs scalar" by `FINDSTRUCT > 0` must get a type-param escape
hatch, or generic code inherits struct ABI. Gate: `_generics_fn_i64_gate`
(check.sh 104→105) compiles `tests/fixtures/monomorph/generic_fn_i64.cyr` under
CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1 (must exit 42) and asserts it's REJECTED with the flag off (the
`: T` return is gate-only) — so the feature provably can't touch default codegen.
Verified byte-correct on x86_64 + aarch64 (qemu). cycc itself uses no generics →
self-host + the differential vs the pre-arc compiler stay byte-identical; the gate
is the only signal.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke"
description = "v6.3.10 added non-i64 generic instances (foo<i32>, Box<Point>) via an instantiate-once engine that RE-INVOKES the canonical emitter on the base's verbatim tokens rather than replicating it. At a non-i64 generic call/use: mint base$ty into the name pool (_mint_generic_name, '$' separator), FINDFN/FINDSTRUCT is the dedup index (hit → reuse the instance fi/sid). On a miss, _instantiate_generic_fn jumps over the instance (EJMP0), STIs to the base fn's `fn`-keyword token (_fnt_defstart, stored +1 because token 0 is valid), sets _inst_name = mangled + _inst_conc0/1 = the concrete tokens, and calls PARSE_FN_DEF — which re-parses the full signature (params/masks/return) + body and emits the specialized fn with T bound to the concretes (PARSE_FN_DEF honors _inst_name for the fn name + _inst_conc0 for the body binding instead of base-as-i64). Enclosing fn state is saved/restored around the re-invoke + _cur_fn_has_closure=1 makes the enclosing fn skip its regalloc byte-scan (the instance is inline in its code range). Generic STRUCTS are the same idea but simpler (no codegen): _instantiate_generic_struct re-invokes PARSE_STRUCT_DEF, whose field `: T` parse gained a _tp_resolve consult."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "instantiate-once", "re-invoke", "PARSE_FN_DEF", "PARSE_STRUCT_DEF", "dedup", "v6.3.10"]
body = """
**Re-invoke, don't replicate.** The clean way to emit a specialized generic
instance is NOT to hand-roll a parallel prologue/param/body emitter — it's to
re-run the CANONICAL emitter (PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF) on the base's
verbatim tokens with two overrides: a NAME override (`_inst_name` → the mangled
`foo$ty` / `Box$ty`) and a BINDING override (`_inst_conc0/1` → the concrete type
tokens, vs the default base-as-i64 `T→i64`). That buys full fidelity — params,
ABI masks, struct-typed args, return type, regalloc — for free, and stays in
lockstep with the base by construction.

**Mechanism (fns).** `_instantiate_generic_fn`: mint → `FINDFN` dedup → on miss,
`EJMP0` over the instance, `STI` to `GFDS(base_fi)` (the base's `fn` token), set
the overrides, `PARSE_FN_DEF(S)`, clear overrides, `EPATCH` the jump. The instance
is emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn's code range (jumped over), so the enclosing
fn must skip its regalloc post-pass — set `_cur_fn_has_closure = 1` in the restore
(the same guard closures use, for the same reason). Save/restore the enclosing
`_cur_fn_*` + `GFLC`/`GLVAR`/`rp_vec`/`GTI` + the local-table snapshot
(`_cl_save_locals`) around the re-invoke.

**THE gotcha — def-start +1.** The base's `fn`-keyword token index is stored as
`_defstart_ti + 1` (and read back `- 1`), because **token index 0 is valid** (a
generic fn can be the very first token in a no-include file) and `0` is the
table's "unrecorded" sentinel. Storing the raw index 0 made the instantiate
return the not-recorded error and every instance silently fell back to a clean
compile error / wrong path until the +1 sentinel fix. Same pattern any time a
0-based index shares a table with a 0=absent sentinel.

**Structs.** Generic structs over i64 already worked (the base struct IS the i64
instance: `val: T` registers as an untyped 8-byte field). Non-i64 use
(`Box<Point>`) re-invokes `PARSE_STRUCT_DEF` (no codegen — struct registration
only, so no `EJMP0`/state-save), with the field-type parse gaining a `_tp_resolve`
consult so `: T` resolves to the concrete sid/width via `ADDFIELDTYPED`. Wired at
the `var x: Box<conc>` type-resolution site. STRUCTSZ/FIELDOFF are lazy, so the
re-added concrete-typed fields yield correct sizes/offsets with no cache work — a
field after a `Holder<Point>`'s 16-byte Point field lands at offset 16, proving
the specialized layout.

**LESSON.** When you need to emit a specialized copy of something the compiler
already knows how to emit, re-invoke the canonical path with overrides — far less
code + drift than a parallel emitter. Verified x86 + aarch64; gated `_MONOMORPH_OK`
→ self-host + differential byte-identical; `_generics_full_gate` (check.sh 106).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_futures_dce_nameread"
description = "v6.3.11 added async/await as first-class FUTURES (deferred-then-forced), NOT stackless CPS coroutines — a premise-check found the cooperative runtime (lib/async.cyr) is run-to-completion (async_run calls fncall1 to completion with blocking epoll yields; no suspend/resume), so true CPS coroutines would need a poll-runtime rework. An `async fn f(p0..pn): T` compiles to a CONSTRUCTOR that allocs a heap Future [ &f$impl, argc, args… ] and returns it (the body becomes a hidden f$impl reached only by pointer; the CALL DOES NOT RUN THE BODY); await fut lowers to future_force(fut) which calls the bundled impl with the bundled args via fncallN. The Future object reuses the closure-env heap construction (alloc + fn-addr fixup + field stores). THE LANDMINE: the pass-2 top-level dispatch reads the fn name at TOKVAL(GTI+1) for its DCE reachability pre-check, assuming the cursor is at `fn` — a leading `async` (token 134) shifted it by one, so it read `fn`(value 0) not the name, mis-marked the async fn unreachable, and stubbed it (call site saw the fn as undefined). Fix: consume `async` INSIDE the fn-dispatch block (cursor → `fn`, so all DCE/match-peek/stub logic stays correct) and arm _pending_async only on the reachable path."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["async", "await", "futures", "future_force", "DCE", "dispatch", "run-to-completion", "v6.3.11"]
body = """
**Futures, not coroutines.** `async fn` / `await` are sugar over the EXISTING
cooperative epoll runtime — and that runtime is run-to-completion (`async_run`
drives each task to completion via `fncall1` with *blocking* epoll/timerfd
yields; it has no suspend/resume). So the roadmap's "CPS state machines" framing
is aspirational for this runtime. The faithful, complete-for-this-runtime model
is first-class **Futures**: calling an `async fn` builds a deferred Future
`[ &f$impl, argc, args… ]` (a heap object — same construction as a closure env);
`await` forces it. True stackless coroutines that suspend/resume *mid-body* across
an `await` are a pinned follow-on needing a poll-based runtime rework. Don't try to
bolt suspend/resume onto the run-to-completion driver.

**Lowering.** In PARSE_FN_DEF, after name-mangling, rename the body to `f$impl`
(`_mint_impl_name`) so the body emits as the hidden impl; at the END (before
`return 0`), `_async_emit_constructor` synthesizes the user-named `f`:
EJMP0 jump-over → EFNPRO → ESTOREPARM the params → ESUBRSP → alloc((2+pc)*8) →
store &f$impl@[0] (ELOAD_FN_ADDR fixup), pc@[8], params@[16+i*8] → return obj →
EFNEPI → EPATCHFRAME → EPATCH. `await` is a factor-level prefix (token 135) in
PARSE_FACTOR: eval operand → rax, EPUSHR, ECALLPOPS(1), ECALLFIX(future_force).
`future_force`/`async_spawn_future` live in lib/async.cyr (shared, pure fncallN).

**The DCE name-read landmine (general).** Any modifier keyword placed BEFORE `fn`
collides with the pass-2 dispatch's DCE reachability pre-check, which reads the fn
name at `TOKVAL(GTI+1)` assuming the cursor sits on `fn`. With `async` in front,
it read the wrong token, the bitmap lookup missed, the fn was deemed unreachable
and STUBBED (`xor eax,eax; ret`), and its call site reported the fn undefined.
The robust fix is to consume the modifier INSIDE the fn-dispatch block so the
cursor lands on `fn` before any name-read / match-peek / stub path runs, and to
arm the per-fn flag (`_pending_async`) only on the reachable branch so a
DCE-stubbed instance can't leak it to the next fn.

**Gating.** Tokens 134/135 are lexed UNCONDITIONALLY (no bare `async`/`await`
identifier exists in src/ or lib/, so cycc's token stream is unchanged →
byte-identical self-host); the FEATURE is gated in pass 2 via a lazy
`_ensure_async_gate` (reads `CYRIUS_ASYNC` on first async/await — no per-fork
driver env-read edit). `_IS_FN_KW` (token 32 OR 134) routes the dispatch in all 7
forks. Verified x86 + aarch64 + native-aarch64; check.sh 107; ecb+cass+pi
SELFHOST_OK; differential + seed→cybs→cycc byte-identical.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_async_reactor_esysxlat_epoll_pwait_collision_and_event_offset"
description = "v6.4.42: the aarch64 epoll async reactor (lib/async.cyr) had NEVER worked — TWO aarch64-only ABI bugs, both invisible to x86 self-host and the async gates (which run only on x86), found only by running the reactor fixtures on REAL pi hardware ('found by ports'). BUG 1 (the blocker): sys_epoll_wait issued the aarch64 epoll_pwait number (22) directly, but 22 is ALSO x86 `pipe`, which ESYSXLAT (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr) rewrites to pipe2(x0,0) + x1=0 — so every reactor wait ran pipe2(epfd, 0) → -EFAULT, and _async_step's epoll_wait never returned, hanging the whole reactor. The classic ESYSXLAT stdlib/x86 number-collision (cf. the flock 73↔ppoll, socket 41, getdents 217 collisions). BUG 2 (latent behind #1): the epoll_event `data` field was written/read at byte offset 4 unconditionally — correct only for x86_64 (EPOLL_PACKED, 12-byte struct); aarch64 leaves the struct unpacked (16 bytes, u64 aligned to offset 8), so the task-ptr round-trip through epoll_data landed at the wrong offset. Fix: sys_epoll_wait issues x86 232 (epoll_wait) + a new ESYSXLAT arg-extend entry maps 232 → epoll_pwait(22) with NULL sigmask (mirrors poll 7→ppoll 73); the reactor's epoll_data store/load are #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64-guarded to @8. Both verified end-to-end on pi (all 7 async fixtures → exit 42, first time ever)."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["aarch64", "async", "reactor", "epoll", "epoll_pwait", "ESYSXLAT", "syscall-collision", "EPOLL_PACKED", "found-by-ports", "pi", "v6.4.42"]
body = """
**Two aarch64-only ABI bugs, both self-host-blind.** cycc never issues epoll, and
the async `*_gate` fixtures (programs/checks/main.cyr) compile with the x86
build/cycc and run natively on x86 — so the aarch64 reactor path was NEVER
exercised in CI. It surfaced only by cross-compiling the reactor fixtures to
aarch64 and running them on real pi. Same class as the v6.2.10 socket / v6.2.24
getdents64 / v6.0.68 stat gaps: "the compiler self-hosts on aarch64" does NOT mean
"aarch64 stdlib syscalls work" — self-host only exercises the ~40 syscalls cycc
itself makes.

**BUG 1 — the ESYSXLAT number collision (the blocker).** ESYSXLAT emits, inline at
every aarch64 syscall site, a runtime cmp/b.ne/movz chain that translates the
x86-numbered syscalls the compiler + tools issue into aarch64 numbers. It also has
`cmp x8,#22 → pipe2 + x1=0` (v6.2.27: aarch64 has no bare pipe(2); raw
`syscall(22,&fds)` is x86-canonical pipe → pipe2). But the async stdlib's
sys_epoll_wait issued aarch64 epoll_pwait = **22** directly. So x8=22 (meant as
epoll_pwait) hit the pipe cmp → rewritten to pipe2(x0=epfd, x1=0) → wrote 2 fds to
address `epfd` → **-EFAULT** on every wait. Diagnosed by dumping (epfd, ctl, nrA)
from a pi probe: epoll_create1(20)=ok, epoll_ctl(21)=ok (20/21 not in ESYSXLAT),
epoll_pwait(22)=-14. ONLY 22 collided (timerfd 85/86, socket 198, close 57, read
63 etc. all pass through untranslated). THE RULE (already in CLAUDE.md): an aarch64
stdlib syscall number that collides with an x86 number ESYSXLAT translates is
silently mis-remapped — issue the x86 number + add an ESYSXLAT entry instead of the
bare aarch64 number. Fix: sys_epoll_wait issues x86 **232** (4 args); a new ESYSXLAT
entry `cmp x8,#232; b.ne +16; movz x4,#0; movz x5,#0; movz x8,#22` arg-extends it to
epoll_pwait(epfd,events,maxevents,timeout,sigmask=0,sigsetsize=0). Placed AFTER the
pipe(22) cmp so the x8=22 it produces is not re-caught (the same ordering rule the
flock/at-family entries use). Exactly mirrors the poll 7→ppoll 73 arg-extend.

**BUG 2 — epoll_event data offset (latent behind #1).** `struct epoll_event` is
`__attribute__((packed))` ONLY under `#ifdef __x86_64__` (linux/eventpoll.h
EPOLL_PACKED) → x86 layout is {events@0 u32, data@4 u64} sizeof 12. aarch64 leaves
it unpacked → {events@0, 4 pad, data@8} sizeof 16. lib/async.cyr wrote the task ptr
to epoll_data and read it back on wake at a hardcoded byte offset 4 (correct for
x86 only). On aarch64 the kernel reads/writes data at offset 8, so the round-tripped
`wtask` was garbage. Fix: `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` → @8, else @4, at BOTH the
write (_async_wait_events) and the wake read (_async_step); keep the standalone
async_await_readable/async_timeout helpers consistent. cycc byte-identical (the
guard's x86 branch is the pre-existing code).

**Verification.** x86 cycc byte-identical (ESYSXLAT lives in
main_aarch64.cyr's chain, not main.cyr; the wrapper is DCE-dead in cycc). ecb
(macho, addition is after the `_TARGET_MACHO==2` early-return) + cass (x86 PE
backend) byte-identical. pi self-hosts byte-identical AND runs all 7 async reactor
fixtures → 42. Lesson: to verify an aarch64 stdlib syscall, run a program that
CALLS it on real aarch64 — qemu-user is NOT a substitute here (qemu's own
epoll_pwait emulation returned nothing for a ready fd, masking the real cause).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "monomorph_default_on_flip_gftp_decouple"
description = "v6.4.0 flipped CYRIUS_MONOMORPH default-on (generics on by default; CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts out). It was NOT a one-liner: the precise per-fn signal is GFTP(S, fi) != 0 (generic-fn-type-params; non-zero = the fn is generic), because a generic fn's base AND all its token-replayed instances SHARE the generic fi. Two entangled prerequisites: (1) DECOUPLE monomorph from _INLINE_OK — they shared the inline-CAPTURE gate (_INLINE_OK==1 || _MONOMORPH_OK==1), so flipping re-enabled GENERAL inlining (+25.6% x86 size regression, the v1.11.3 class) → gate capture to `_INLINE_OK==1 || (_MONOMORPH_OK==1 && GFTP(S,fi)!=0)` at BOTH SFBS/SFBE sites; (2) narrow the v6.3.29 frame-trim blanket (`if _MONOMORPH_OK!=0 return`) to `if _MONOMORPH_OK==1 && GFTP(S,fi)!=0 return`, passing fi into _ra_frame_trim. cycc has ZERO generic fns → flag-on == flag-off byte-identical (1,024,552 B). DEAD ENDS (the 'why not X' record): _cur_fn_has_closure (incomplete), _ra_picker_ran (over-conserved +12KB), _inst_conc0 (missed nested). GFTP is the only clean signal — a property of the FN, not transient emit state."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "GFTP", "default-on-flip", "inline-decouple", "frame-trim", "_INLINE_OK", "_MONOMORPH_OK", "v6.4.0"]
body = """
**The flip needed a precise per-fn signal, not a global toggle.** v6.4.0
made `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH` default-ON (`_MONOMORPH_OK = 1` in
`src/common/util.cyr`); `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` is the opt-out. The whole
generics feature was already correct behind the flag (through v6.3.39) —
this slot de-risked the DEFAULT flip, and it was NOT a one-liner. Two
prerequisites were entangled, and both are resolved by the SAME signal:
**`GFTP(S, fi) != 0`** — the fn has generic type-params. A generic fn's
base AND every token-replayed instance share the generic fn's `fi`, so
`GFTP != 0` cleanly covers the whole generic family and NOTHING else
(see the comment at parse_fn.cyr:1834).

**Prerequisite 1 — decouple monomorph from `_INLINE_OK`.** The two
shared the inline-CAPTURE gate: `_INLINE_OK == 1 || _MONOMORPH_OK == 1`.
With `_MONOMORPH_OK` now 1 by default, that OR re-enabled GENERAL value
inlining for EVERY fn — a **+25.6 %** x86 size regression (the same
class as the original v1.11.3 inline-off decision; ~1.02 MB → ~1.29 MB
on a flag-on self-host). Fix: qualify the monomorph arm with GFTP at
BOTH capture sites (SFBS body-start + SFBE classify):
`if (_INLINE_OK == 1 || (_MONOMORPH_OK == 1 && GFTP(S, fi) != 0))`
(parse_fn.cyr:3133 / :3178). Non-generic fns are never captured;
generic fns still are.

**Prerequisite 2 — narrow the v6.3.29 frame-trim blanket.** The frame
trim had a blanket `if (_MONOMORPH_OK != 0) return` — under the flag
that disabled the trim for EVERY fn (+16 KB). Narrow it to
`if (_MONOMORPH_OK == 1 && GFTP(S, fi) != 0) return` and thread `fi`
through — `_ra_frame_trim(S, fn_start, fi)` (parse_fn.cyr:1821, guard
at :1838). Only generic fns skip the trim now.

**Why byte-identical.** cycc's own source has ZERO generic fns, so
under the flip every capture/trim GFTP check is 0 → flag-on codegen ==
flag-off codegen → cycc self-hosts byte-identical (1,024,552 B) and the
differential is status-diff=0 / codegen-diff=0 over the whole corpus in
BOTH modes.

**DEAD ENDS (kept as the 'why not X' record).** Three other per-fn
signals were tried and rejected before GFTP:
  - `_cur_fn_has_closure` — incomplete (doesn't cover all generic fns).
  - `_ra_picker_ran` — over-conserved (+12 KB; too broad).
  - `_inst_conc0` — missed NESTED instances.
GFTP won because it is a PROPERTY OF THE FN (its captured type-params),
not transient emit state that a given pass may or may not have set. The
v6.3.5 `_monomorph_substrate_gate` was REPURPOSED here: it now proves
the DECOUPLE holds (monomorph-on leaves non-generic fns un-inlined), the
inverse of what it originally proved.

**Recognize again.** Any 'flip a gated default on' where the gate shared
an OR with another feature's gate: flipping silently re-enables that
other feature for the whole program. The fix is a precise per-fn (or
per-site) qualifier that is a stable PROPERTY, not transient state — and
measure the size delta on a flag-on self-host, because a same-file-size
cycc (0 generic fns) hides a large regression on real consumers.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "alloc_reset_zero_on_reset_chunk_model_and_lib_fix_not_auto_byte_identical"
description = "v6.4.1 made alloc_reset() zero the reclaimed span (CVE-2026-34988-class memory-reuse info-leak — the ONE point the forward-only bump allocator re-hands an address). THE TRAP: the naive `memset(_heap_first_base, 0, _heap_used)` is UNSAFE — _heap_used is CUMULATIVE across chunks, so it over-runs the first chunk (past _heap_first_end) on a multi-chunk heap. Only the FIRST chunk is a reuse channel (spills re-mmap fresh kernel-zeroed chunks), so scrub its written extent: `_heap_ptr - _heap_first_base` when still in the first chunk (common, exact), else the whole first chunk via the `_heap_base != _heap_first_base` spill discriminator; macOS/Windows single-region backends zero exactly [_heap_base, _heap_ptr). A new un-gated `_alloc_zero(base,n)` (store64/store8 loop) keeps the foundational allocator free of a lib/string.cyr memset dependency; fixed in all 4 backends. DURABLE LESSON: cycc INCLUDES lib/alloc.cyr AND alloc_reset is reachable via the allocator vtable's reset_fn (address-taken → NOT DCE'd), so the scrub added +424 B of code to cycc (absorbed into page-align padding → file size unchanged at 1,024,552 B). A 'lib fix' is NOT automatically cycc-byte-identical when cycc includes the lib."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["alloc", "alloc_reset", "info-leak", "CVE-2026-34988", "chunk-chain", "_heap_used", "_alloc_zero", "lib-fix-byte-identical", "vtable-reachable", "v6.4.1"]
body = """
**The leak.** The bump allocator is forward-only — every allocation
moves onto never-touched, kernel-zeroed pages — EXCEPT `alloc_reset()`,
the one point it rewinds the bump pointer and hands the same address to
a new owner. Pre-v6.4.1 it rewound without zeroing, so a post-reset
allocation read the prior occupant's bytes (CVE-2026-34988 /
CVE-2022-39393 class; sandhi's per-batch reset is the live call site).

**THE TRAP — `_heap_used` is cumulative across chunks.** The obvious
fix `memset(_heap_first_base, 0, _heap_used)` is UNSAFE: on a chunk-chain
allocator (`lib/alloc.cyr` Linux, `alloc_agnos.cyr`) `_heap_used` counts
bytes across ALL chunks, so it over-runs the first chunk — writing past
`_heap_first_end` into whatever follows. Correct model: only the FIRST
chunk is a reuse channel (a spill past it re-`mmap`s a fresh, already
kernel-zeroed chunk that alloc_reset drops entirely), so scrub just the
first chunk's WRITTEN extent (`lib/alloc.cyr:243-257`):

    var span = _heap_ptr - _heap_first_base;                       # still in chunk 1 (common, exact)
    if (_heap_base != _heap_first_base) {                          # a spill happened
        span = _heap_first_end - _heap_first_base;                 # → whole first chunk
    }
    if (span > 0) { _alloc_zero(_heap_first_base, span); }

The `_heap_base != _heap_first_base` test IS the multi-chunk
discriminator. macOS (`alloc_macos.cyr`) / Windows (`alloc_windows.cyr`)
are single contiguous regions → zero exactly `[_heap_base, _heap_ptr)`.

**Why a local `_alloc_zero`.** `alloc.cyr` is the foundational
allocator; making it call `memset` (lib/string.cyr) would give every
platform backend a string.cyr dependency. New un-gated
`_alloc_zero(base, n)` (alloc.cyr:105) is a plain `store64`/`store8`
loop, visible to every included backend (the `arena_*` precedent proves
un-gated alloc.cyr fns reach the backends). Fixed in ALL FOUR backends.

**THE DURABLE LESSON — a lib fix is not auto cycc-byte-identical when
cycc includes the lib.** cycc INCLUDES `lib/alloc.cyr`, AND `alloc_reset`
is reachable via the allocator vtable's `reset_fn` (address-taken → NOT
DCE-eliminated). So the scrub added **+424 B of code to cycc** — here it
happened to be absorbed into page-align padding (file size unchanged at
1,024,552 B), but that is luck, not a guarantee. Do NOT assume 'it's
just a lib change → cycc is byte-identical'; run the self-host fixpoint
(and, for any allocator/heap change, the two-step + seed-derive) for a
lib touched by code cycc includes.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_neon_simd_emitter_mirror_f64v_loop_verify_encodings_with_llvm_mc_and_value_form_params_die_on_win64"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
Implementing an aarch64 NEON SIMD emitter (SIMD Phase 5 — EMIT_F32V_LOOP/FMADD/DOT,
and the pending EMIT_IVEC_*/EMIT_F32V8_*) is far easier than it looks because the
loop + addressing + reduce infrastructure already exists for f64: mirror
`EMIT_F64V_LOOP` / `EMIT_F64V_DOT` in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr. The pattern:
`mov x10,x0`(n) `movz x11,#0`(index); loop `cmp x11,x10`/`b.ge done`; per operand
`EFLLOAD(slot)` → `mov x12,x0` → `add x12,x12,x11,lsl #K` → `ldr q, [x12]`; the NEON
op; `str q, [x12]`; `add x11,x11,#LANES`; `EJMP`/`EPATCH`. To go from f64v (2 lanes)
to f32v4 (4 lanes) change exactly three things:
  - element stride: `lsl #3` (×8) → `lsl #2` (×4)   [encoding 0x8B0B0D8C → 0x8B0B098C]
  - the NEON op:    `.2d` → `.4s`  (fadd .4s=0x4E21D400, fsub=0x4EA1D400, fmul=0x6E21DC00)
  - counter:        `add x11,#2` → `add x11,#4`      [0x9100096B → 0x9100116B]
The 128-bit `ldr q`/`str q` are identical (width-agnostic). Horizontal reduce: f64
needs ONE `faddp d0,v2.2d`; f32v4 needs TWO `faddp v2.4s` (4→2→1) then `fmov w0,s2`.

THREE traps:
(1) VERIFY EVERY hand-encoded word with `llvm-mc --arch=aarch64 --show-encoding`
    (`printf 'fadd v0.4s,v0.4s,v1.4s\\n...' | llvm-mc ...`). Do NOT hand-compute A64
    encodings from memory — the imm6 shift field + the sz bit are easy to get wrong.
(2) MATCH x86 ROUNDING for fmadd/dot: x86 SSE has no FMA at f32v4 width, so its
    EMIT_F32V_FMADD/DOT do mulps THEN addps (two roundings). Use `fmul`+`fadd`, NOT
    the fused `fmla`, so the two arches agree bit-for-bit on the test values. (The
    pre-existing f64 dot DOES use fmla and already diverges cross-arch — don't copy
    that for new f32 work.)
(3) HISTORICAL (fixed v6.4.31): value-form f32v4/f64v2/f64v4 PARAMS/RETURNS once
    hard-errored on Win64 PE ("value-form params not yet supported on Win64 PE —
    use pointer-form"). As of v6.4.31 they are address-passed (by hidden pointer) on
    Win64 — parse_decl.cyr "v6.4.31: value-form SIMD arg → address-passed on Win64",
    and main_win.cyr PP_PREDEFINEs CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS — so a value-form SIMD
    test now runs on cass and CAN be a full `vr01_` cross-OS gate. (When first landing
    an aarch64 SIMD emitter the strict-XFAIL machinery in ci.yml still auto-forces XFAIL
    removal: the corpus gate is `test $xpass -eq 0`, so the test flips RED (XPASS) the
    moment your emitter lands — that RED is the cue to remove it from XFAIL, not a
    regression.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cx_simd_emitters_are_easy_the_real_bugs_are_local_addressing_eloadlocaladdr_stub_and_regalloc_disp_nonuniformity"
description = "cx SIMD (f64v_*/f32v_*/iv_*) emitters mirror the f64 loop trivially; the real blockers are 2 pre-existing cx local-addressing bugs the SIMD pointer-stash exposes: ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR was a return-0 stub (&local aliases to addr 0) and the scalar load/store disp path omitted the regalloc/ret_stash reservation EFLADDR already had. Plus cxvm >> is LOGICAL."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "cxvm", "simd", "codegen", "regalloc", "frame-layout", "v6.4.32"]
content = """
Adding SIMD to the cx bytecode backend (`src/backend/cx/emit.cyr`) is the same
"mirror the f64 loop" job as the aarch64 NEON one — a single `_CX_VLOOP_BIN` driver
covers f64v/f32v/iv add/sub/mul/div, and the special verbs (dot/scale/axpy/fmadd/
sqrt/dp8) are short variants. cx is a scalar register VM, so a "SIMD" op is just a
per-lane loop; it's a byte-exact **correctness oracle**, not hardware SIMD. Three
cx-specific encoding facts:

- **n arrives in r0, NOT a frame slot.** The intrinsic dispatch leaves the count in
  r0 (like x86 rax after the arg stash); every emitter's first instruction is
  `mov r8, r0` to save it before r0 is reused for values. A `nslot` param on the
  loop driver is a trap — there is no such slot.
- **f32 = compute-in-f64, round per lane.** Widen both operands (new cxvm opcode
  `f32widen` 0x66 = cvtss2sd), run the scalar op in f64, narrow the result
  (`f32narrow` 0x67 = cvtsd2ss). One packed op → one rounding, bit-exact vs `addps`/
  `mulps`. `fmadd` narrows twice to match `mulps`+`addps` (two roundings). `f64v_sqrt`
  compiles to the new `fsqrt` 0x68.
- **cxvm `>>` (op 0x24) is LOGICAL, not arithmetic.** So the usual sign-extend trick
  `(x << 56) >> 56` does NOT work for the i8 lane in `EMIT_IVEC_DP8`. Use
  `b − ((b >> 7) << 8)` instead (subtract 256 iff the sign bit is set); `a` stays
  zero-extended, accumulate in i64.

**But the emitters are the easy part — SIMD is a tripwire for two PRE-EXISTING cx
local-addressing bugs** that never bit before because nothing else stashed pointers
in frame slots next to stack arrays:

1. **`ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` was a `return 0` stub.** `&local` emitted nothing and left
   r0 = 0, so `&a` and `&b` for two different locals BOTH evaluated to address 0 — a
   silent aliasing bug for *any* address-of-a-stack-variable, not just SIMD. It only
   surfaced as "f64v_add(&R,&A,&B,n) writes garbage" because SIMD is the first common
   caller of `&local`. Fix: emit `r0 = fp − adisp`.
2. **cx local-slot disp non-uniformity.** `EFLADDR` (address-of) folded the
   `_cur_fn_regalloc` + `_cur_fn_ret_stash` reservation into its slot disp, but the
   SCALAR load/store path (`EFLLOAD`/`EFLSTORE`, the f64v2 load/store pairs,
   `ESTOREPARM`, and the SIMD stash loader `_cx_ld_local`) did NOT. A fn using both
   stack arrays (addressed via EFLADDR) and the SIMD pointer stash (stored via
   EFLSTORE) had the stash slots land on top of regalloc-shifted array storage. Fix:
   route EVERY local-disp site through one `_cx_ldisp(idx)` helper that always adds
   the reservation. Lesson: when one addressing path folds a frame reservation, ALL
   of them must — an address-of and a load of the same slot index must resolve to the
   same byte.

**Debugging method that separated regression from pre-existing (reusable):**
- Isolate SIMD correctness with synthetic **manual exit-code** probes (`if (x != y)
  syscall(60, code)`), NOT assert_eq — library code drags in its own frame/global
  behavior and muddies the signal.
- To prove regression-vs-pre-existing, `git stash` the `src/` change and rebuild the
  cx toolchain (`build/cycc < src/main_cx.cyr > cycc_cx`; `build/cycc < programs/
  cxvm.cyr > cxvm`) — `build/cycc` is the committed prior-release x86 binary,
  unchanged by your edit, so it reproduces the exact prior-release cx behavior. Run
  the SAME repro against it. Here it proved the frame fix has NO general `var x =
  <call>` regression with large (4-array) frames, and split off a genuinely
  pre-existing cx bug: `var x = assert_summary()` mis-captures after a prior
  `assert_eq` mutated the assert globals (fails on the prior release, zero SIMD/
  arrays). Consequence: the standard tcyr ending `var r = assert_summary();` is
  unreliable on cx — end cx gates with `return assert_summary();` directly.

**Verifying cross-OS:** the cx round-trip fixture in `scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh` is
built by the LOCAL cycc_cx (shipped `.cyx`) AND recompiled by each host's NATIVE
cycc_cx, then run on the native cxvm. Upgrade it from a bare write to run a real SIMD
op (`f64v_add`+`f64v_sqrt` over local arrays → exit 42) so a green ecb/pi/cass leg
proves the cx SIMD emitters + frame fix + new opcodes execute on real hardware — not
the placebo "a write halts" that CLAUDE.md warns about.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_auto_calls_main_top_level_var_main_double_runs_and_the_execve_helper_dispatch_pattern"
description = "Three program-shape gotchas surfaced building the `cyrsign-efi` UEFI helper (v6.4.47). (1) DOUBLE-RUN: cyrius AUTO-CALLS `fn main` at program end, so a file that ALSO has a top-level `var z = main();` runs main TWICE (the crypto helper printed its output twice). Fix: end such a program with `var _rc = main(); syscall(60, _rc);` — the explicit exit syscall fires BEFORE the auto-main call, so main runs exactly once and the process exits with its rc. (2) args_init FIRST: `argc()`/`argv()` read a buffer `args_init()` fills from /proc/self/cmdline (lib/args.cyr, under `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`) — call `args_init()` before any argc/argv or you get argc 0 / garbage argv. (3) HELPER-DISPATCH: `cyrius sign-efi` does NOT link sigil's ~14 MB of crypto into the CLI — it `sys_execve`s a SEPARATE `cyrsign-efi` binary (located via find_tools/_tools_dir), keeping the CLI delta +144 B. The `[bins]` manifest in cyrius.cyml drives install.sh building the helper (`_build_tool sign-efi programs/cyrsign-efi.cyr`). ARGV-ARRAY SIZE trap: `var _se_argv: i64[16]` = 16 SLOTS (correct); `var _se_argv[16]` = 16 BYTES (2 slots) and overflows the execve argv. Windows `sys_execve` is a stub returning -1, so the dispatcher is Linux/macOS only."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["main", "auto-call", "double-run", "args_init", "argv", "execve", "helper-dispatch", "cyrsign-efi", "sign-efi", "var-array-bytes-vs-slots", "v6.4.47"]

[[entries]]
name = "capacity_warnings_were_driver_fork_duplicated_now_one_shared_capacity_warnings_helper_all_7_drivers"
description = "v6.4.50 closed the gap the v6.4.49 note ends on: the end-of-compile soft capacity warnings (`warning: fn_table/identifier buffer/var_table/fixup_table/string_data at 85%`, `code buffer at 75%`) lived INLINE and byte-identically DUPLICATED in only 2 of the 7 compiler drivers — main.cyr (x86-Linux) + main_win.cyr (Win64). The other 5 — main_aarch64{,_native,_macho}.cyr, main_x86_macho.cyr, main_cx.cyr — emitted ZERO capacity warnings, which is why a consumer's codebuf warning (thoth) only ever showed on x86 and never on the macOS/aarch64 toolchains. FIX: extract the block into ONE shared `fn _capacity_warnings(S, codebuf_cap, codebuf_growable)` in common/util.cyr (next to _codebuf_grow), called from every driver's end-of-compile path. KEY DESIGN POINTS for anyone touching it: (1) it reads CYRIUS_STATS itself via `_read_env` — which has TWO definitions, the real one in backend/common/runtime.cyr (native forks) and a `return 0` STUB in backend/cx/emit.cyr (cx fork excludes runtime.cyr), so the call resolves in ALL forks (a util.cyr FORWARD call to _read_env is fine — direct calls resolve via the fixup table regardless of include order; only _FINDFN_CSTR runtime lookups need pre-registration). (2) FORK-AGNOSTIC: every metric goes through its accessor (GFNC/GNPOS/GVCNT/GFCNT/GSPOS/GCP) — no hardcoded S+offset — and the fixed table caps (8192/262144/8192/1048576/2097152) are identical across all forks. (3) The ONLY fork-varying line is the code-buffer warning: the 6 NATIVE forks pass `(67108864, 1)` — the 64 MiB growable off-heap codebuf, message '…% of 64 MiB ceiling (N bytes, +8 MiB/grow)' (byte-identical to the old inline text); cx passes `(524288, 0)` — its code lives in the SEPARATE fixed 512 KiB region at 0x54A000 (the shared _codebuf_base/_codebuf_cap it sets to 3 MiB are VESTIGIAL for cx), message '…% of the 512 KiB cx region (N bytes)' with no grow advice. cx had NO overflow guard on that region, so this is its first heads-up. (4) INSERTION POINT matters: call it AFTER FIXUP(S) (GCP is final) and BEFORE EMITELF/output-write; on cx the warnings go to stderr (fd 2) and the .cyx to stdout (fd 1), so they don't corrupt output. VERIFICATION (this is a logic-preserving DRIVER code-move, NOT a codegen change): cycc self-host fixpoint byte-identical, seed-derive gen2==gen3==build/cycc (cybs compiled the ~30-call helper cleanly — watch for the cybs per-fn call-reference limit, but it held), differential corpus 0/0 in BOTH default and DCE modes, dead-code floor unchanged (64/24633), x86 behavioral: the var_table warning fires with byte-identical text old-vs-new AND the compiled binary is byte-identical. Cross-OS pi+ecb+cass all SELFHOST_OK + LIBTEST_OK. NOTE cx's var_table warning (85% of 8192) is UNREACHABLE — cx hard-errors at 4096 initialized globals first — so the reachable cx warning is the code-buffer one. See CHANGELOG [6.4.50], roadmap Pin 3."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["capacity-warning", "driver-fork", "_capacity_warnings", "util.cyr", "read_env-dual-definition", "cx-separate-region", "fork-agnostic", "logic-preserving", "differential-corpus", "v6.4.50"]

[[entries]]
name = "output_buf_1gib_alloc_caps_on_macos_windows_linux_overcommits_platform_adaptive_output_cap_fallback"
description = "v6.4.51 raised output_buf 16 MiB→1 GiB by moving it OFF-HEAP (`_output_base = alloc(1073741824)`, routed through ~19 emit/fixup/write sites via a `_output_base` seam in common/util.cyr, replacing the fixed S+0x4D9D000 region). It works on Linux but cycc SIGSEGV'd on real macOS (ecb) + failed on Windows (cass) — because the macOS and Windows ALLOCATORS CANNOT PRODUCE A >256 MiB SINGLE REGION. macOS (lib/alloc_macos.cyr) caps at its 256 MiB initial reservation: growth uses a HINTED 1 MiB mmap at _heap_end, macOS does NOT honor mmap address hints, so the first grow past the reservation returns a different base and the contiguity guard fails → alloc returns 0. Windows (lib/alloc_windows.cyr) has a FIXED reservation with NO grow (`if (new_ptr > _heap_end) return 0`). Only Linux (lib/alloc.cyr) handles it — `_linux_new_chunk` mmaps a fresh chunk SIZED TO THE REQUEST and Linux overcommits anon mmap. When alloc(1 GiB) returns 0, `_output_base` was 0 → cycc emits its output image to address 0 → SIGSEGV. FIX (ship-Linux-now, .52-for-the-rest): PLATFORM-ADAPTIVE fallback — each of the 6 native drivers does `_output_base = alloc(1073741824); if (_output_base == 0) { _output_base = S+0x4D9D000; _output_cap = 16777216; } else { _output_cap = 1073741824; }`, and ALL the output-size caps (`_check_output_cap` in runtime.cyr + the x86/fixup.cyr ELF-exe `final_sz`/`.so`/`.o` caps — there are FOUR, not one; a >16 MiB binary trips whichever fires first) key on the `_output_cap` GLOBAL, never a hardcoded literal. So Linux gets 1 GiB, macOS/Windows keep the pre-.51 16 MiB (no crash, no regression). The dedicated macOS/Windows large-alloc path (a direct unhinted mmap on macOS / dedicated VirtualAlloc on Windows, mirroring _linux_new_chunk) is v6.4.52 — see issue 2026-07-11-macos-windows-large-single-allocation-path.md. LESSON: raising ALLOC_MAX (the size guard) does NOT make a large alloc succeed if the underlying per-OS allocator physically can't produce the region — the guard and the mechanism are different things. TWO test-harness traps hit while verifying this: (1) `cyrius build` uses the INSTALLED toolchain cycc (~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/bins/cycc), NOT ./build/cycc — so a COMPILER change (caps, codegen) must be tested with raw `cat prog.cyr | build/cycc` (or reinstall); `cyrius build` is only valid for LIB changes. A >16 MiB test 'failing' with the old cap was just the stale installed cycc. (2) `cyrius build` takes output as a POSITIONAL arg (`cyrius build <src> <out>`), NOT `-o` — `cyrius build src -o out` silently creates a file literally named `-o` and ignores `out`. See CHANGELOG [6.4.51]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["output_buf", "alloc", "macos", "windows", "overcommit", "mmap-hint", "platform-adaptive", "_output_cap", "cyrius-build-uses-installed-cycc", "cyrius-build-positional-output", "multiple-output-caps", "v6.4.51"]

[[entries]]
name = "signal_ignore_sigpipe_macho_esysxlat_rt_sigaction_to_bsd_sigaction_46_socketpair_test_per_arch_nums"
description = "v6.4.51 added `signal_ignore(signum)` (lib/syscalls.cyr) so a server on `sock_send` survives a peer disconnect (SIGPIPE) instead of being killed — the sandhi macOS DoS gap. ONE fn, target-gated by #ifdef (exactly one branch survives per compile, so the #ifdef-blind LSP wrongly warns 'duplicate fn' — ignore it; grep confirms one def): Linux rt_sigaction (x86 #13 / aarch64 #134, 4-arg incl. sigsetsize=8), macOS BSD sigaction (3-arg), Win/agnos no-op. The 32-byte {sa_handler=SIG_IGN,0,0,0} struct is valid for BOTH the Linux and BSD sigaction ABIs. MACOS is the whole point (Linux had sandhi's raw-syscall workaround; macOS had none): it needed the Mach-O ESYSXLAT to translate rt_sigaction → BSD sigaction 46 — aarch64 `cmp x8,#134`(0xF102191F)/`movz x16,#46`(0xD28005D0, llvm-mc-verified) + THE PARALLEL `_macho_arm_routes` MIRROR LIST (add 134 there too or parse_expr's macho whitelist warning drifts), and x86 `_msx(S, 13, 0x200002E)`. cycc doesn't include syscalls.cyr so the lib part is self-host-neutral; the ESYSXLAT entries are macho-backend-only (x86-Linux differential 0). VERIFIED ON REAL ecb via tests/tcyr/crossos/signal_ignore.tcyr: socketpair → close one end → write → assert EPIPE (not SIGPIPE-kill). TEST-WRITING TRAP that cost a cross-OS round-trip: a vr01 test that raw-`syscall(53/3/1,...)`s HARDCODES x86 numbers and SIGSEGVs on aarch64/macOS (53 = fchmodat on the aarch64 enum) — use the PER-ARCH `SYS_SOCKETPAIR`/`SYS_CLOSE`/`SYS_WRITE` constants (x86 53/3/1, aarch64 199/57/64, macOS via ESYSXLAT) so the numbers are right on every target; qemu-aarch64 catches the aarch64 case locally before the pi leg. Also: a .tcyr must end `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);` (explicit exit; cyrius auto-main otherwise returns a GARBAGE exit code) AND include assert+alloc+string+fmt+syscalls (assert alone → 'undefined main'). See CHANGELOG [6.4.51], issue 2026-07-11-sandhi-signal-ignore-stdlib-gap.md."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["signal_ignore", "sigpipe", "sigaction", "esysxlat", "macho", "_macho_arm_routes", "rt_sigaction", "per-arch-syscall-nums", "vr01-test", "tcyr-explicit-exit", "duplicate-fn-lsp-false-positive", "v6.4.51"]

[[entries]]
name = "macos_windows_large_alloc_path_dedicated_unhinted_mmap_and_virtualalloc_output_buf_1gib_all_platforms_v6452"
description = "v6.4.52 SOLVED the macOS/Windows allocator wall from v6.4.51 (see the sibling note about alloc(1 GiB) failing there): give each allocator a dedicated LARGE-object path, mirroring Linux's fresh-chunk-per-request. macOS (lib/alloc_macos.cyr alloc()): for a request that won't fit the remaining 256 MiB reserve (`if (_heap_ptr + size > _heap_end)`), do a dedicated UNHINTED `syscall(9, 0, size, 3, 0x1002, 0-1, 0)` (mmap(NULL,size,RW,MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON)) and RETURN IT DIRECTLY — bump pointer untouched, no contiguity needed. This REPLACES the old hinted 1 MiB grow loop, which could never succeed on macOS (macOS ignores mmap address hints without MAP_FIXED, so the grow returned a non-hint base and the contiguity guard `result != _heap_end` always failed). macOS overcommits anonymous mmap (proven: alloc_init already unhinted-mmaps the 256 MiB reserve, and main_aarch64_macho.cyr unhinted-mmaps its ~94 MiB heap, both self-host on ecb every release), so a 1 GiB mapping costs only touched pages. NO ESYSXLAT change needed — syscall(9) already translates 9→BSD mmap 197 (aarch64 emit.cyr) / 0x20000C5 (x86 EMACHO_SYSXLAT). Windows (lib/alloc_windows.cyr alloc()): replace the no-grow bail `if (new_ptr > _heap_end) return 0` with a dedicated `syscall(9, 0, size, 3, 0x22, 0-1, 0)` which the PE backend reroutes via EMMAP_PE (parse_expr.cyr literal-syscall(9) path -> src/backend/x86/emit.cyr:1879) to VirtualAlloc(NULL,size,MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE,PAGE_READWRITE). EAGER-COMMIT CAVEAT: EMMAP_PE hardcodes `mov r8d,0x3000` (MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE) — there is NO MEM_RESERVE-only variant in the backend — so a 1 GiB VirtualAlloc charges the full 1 GiB against the Windows commit limit (RAM+pagefile) UP FRONT (physical RAM still lazy/demand-zero). This SUCCEEDS on a normal dev box (verified on cass; cass's own ~116 MiB heap at main_win.cyr:351 commits up front every release) but a reserve-only + commit-on-touch path would need a NEW codegen path (a fault/commit-on-write seam) — deferred. alloc_reset SAFE on both: dedicated large mappings live outside the bump [_heap_base,_heap_ptr) region, are fresh kernel-zeroed, never recycled (no reuse info-leak). Then the 6 native drivers drop the .51 `if (_output_base == 0) { ...S+0x4D9D000; _output_cap=16777216 }` fallback: `_output_base = alloc(1073741824); if (_output_base == 0) { error+exit }; _output_cap = 1073741824;` — a DEFENSIVE GUARD (hard-error on true OOM, NOT a blind assign that SIGSEGVs writing to page 0). _output_cap stays the single source of truth for all 4 output caps. VERIFY: tests/tcyr/crossos/alloc_1gib.tcyr (alloc 1 GiB + touch both ends, per-arch SYS_* not raw nums) in the cross-OS vr01_ glob — must PASS on real ecb + cass + pi, not just self-host. This is a LIB+driver change so cycc x86-Linux codegen is differential-0, but cycc INCLUDES the driver so its size shifts (+48 B). See CHANGELOG [6.4.52], issue archived/2026-07-11-macos-windows-large-single-allocation-path.md."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["alloc", "macos", "windows", "large-allocation", "unhinted-mmap", "virtualalloc", "mem-commit", "eager-commit", "emmap-pe", "output_buf", "1gib-all-platforms", "alloc-reset-safe", "vr01_alloc_1gib", "v6.4.52"]

[[entries]]
name = "sankoch_batch_gzip_reencoded_up_to_258_bytes_at_every_1mib_block_boundary_and_every_test_input_was_smaller"
description = "v6.4.79 folded sankoch 2.7.6, fixing silent data corruption in `gzip_compress` / `deflate_compress` / `deflate_compress_level` for **any input larger than `DEFLATE_BLOCK_SIZE` (1 MiB)**. Both per-block encoders deliberately match against the FULL `src` rather than just their own slice — cross-block back-refs are what keeps the ratio — so a match beginning just below `block_end` can extend up to `LZ77_MAX_MATCH` (258) bytes PAST it. The outer chunker then resumed the next block at `block_end` regardless, encoding every overshoot byte a SECOND time. Nothing errored at compress time; the corruption surfaced arbitrarily later as a CRC-32 failure. In the consumer (stiva) that meant **every container image over ~1 MiB was written to disk corrupt** — `import` succeeded, `run` failed with `layer unpack error`; 849 KB worked, 1.69 MB did not. Both Huffman paths were affected (fixed, levels 1-3; dynamic, 4-9), so lowering the level was never an escape. The STREAMING encoder never had the bug — `_denc_consume` stores the offset its LZ77 loop actually reached — so the fix gives the batch path that same contract: each block encoder PUBLISHES where it really stopped and the chunker resumes there. **WHY ~160K ASSERTIONS MISSED IT FOR YEARS: every pre-existing deflate/gzip test used an input SMALLER than the block size** — the largest was 80,000 bytes — so the outer chunker never took a second iteration and the boundary was never crossed. A shipping consumer found it instead. DURABLE RULE for any chunked / blocked / paged encoder: the corpus must include an input that CROSSES the boundary, one that lands EXACTLY on it, and one whose tail is shorter than the maximum unit — and it must assert **byte-exactness, not length**, because a length check passes on a stream that duplicated N bytes and dropped N others. TWO SUBTLETIES that would each have left a partial fix, worth carrying to any similar encoder: the fixed path's trailing lazy-match flush writes a match STARTING at `sp - 1`, so the consumed end is `sp - 1 + prev_match`, not `sp` (missing it leaves the duplication for lazy-matched input alone — the hardest variant to notice); and `BFINAL` is decided from `block_end` BEFORE the block is encoded, so a block that now consumes through to `src_len` yields a stream with every byte encoded but NO block carrying `BFINAL`, which a decoder considers truncated (closed with an empty final fixed block, header + EOB, ~10 bits — legal since DEFLATE permits mixing block types). METHOD: GNU `gunzip` cross-validated it, having been rejecting the same stream with `differ: char 1048797` — an external decoder is the cheapest way to isolate a codec bug to the ENCODER. And it was fixed UPSTREAM first (a real sankoch 2.7.6 release: VERSION + CHANGELOG + dist regen + full suite) and only then folded, per 'fix the SOURCE repo, not the fold' — a patch applied only to `lib/sankoch.cyr` evaporates at the next re-vendor. See CHANGELOG [6.4.79]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["sankoch", "gzip", "deflate", "block-boundary", "lz77", "LZ77_MAX_MATCH", "silent-corruption", "boundary-crossing-test", "byte-exactness-not-length", "bfinal", "lazy-match-flush", "gnu-gunzip-crossvalidate", "fix-the-source-repo-not-the-fold", "v6.4.79"]

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_distlib_regenerates_only_the_main_bundle_the_sub_profiles_keep_the_old_code_under_a_fresh_version_string"
description = "`cyrius distlib` rebuilds **exactly ONE bundle per invocation** and offers no way to ask the manifest which profiles exist. Cutting sankoch 2.7.6 (v6.4.79) showed what that means: the documented release step regenerated `dist/sankoch.cyr` to 2.7.6 and left **all NINE sub-profiles at 2.7.5, still carrying the buggy encoder** — including `dist/sankoch-gzip.cyr`, the profile whose entire purpose is the codec that had just been fixed. Nothing in the tool or the release script says a word; it was caught only by sweeping version strings across `dist/*.cyr` afterwards. So every multi-profile repo hand-maintains its profile list in two more places, and **both hand-rolled CI loops in the ecosystem have already drifted from their manifests**: sankoch's covers 7 of 9 (`zip` and `zipall` are tracked, shipped bundles CI never regenerates OR diff-checks — the staleness check omits them too), sigil's covers 12 of 13 (missing `argon2`). Exposure is 36 sub-profiles across 6 repos (sigil 13, sankoch 9, bayan 8, sandhi 4, yukti 1, vani 1). PRACTICAL RULE until `--all` / `--check` exist (filed): after ANY upstream fix, regenerate every profile and then VERIFY by extracting the version header out of each `dist/*.cyr` — do not trust the CI loop, and never read a fresh version string on the main bundle as evidence the profiles moved. **A fresh version string on a stale bundle is worse than an old one**, because it defeats the obvious check. RELATED TRAP from the same sweep: there are **THREE different bundle header formats** in the ecosystem, so a single-pattern version grep silently SKIPS repos — sakshi uses `# Bundled distribution of sakshi vX` rather than `# Version:`, which is exactly how an earlier sweep reported the fold table verified while never having looked at that row. `docs/ecosystem.md`'s folded-distlib table had drifted on 5 of 11 rows for the same reason; verify it against the live `lib/*.cyr` headers, with a pattern per format. See CHANGELOG [6.4.77], [6.4.79]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "dist-profiles", "sub-profiles", "sankoch", "sigil", "stale-bundle", "fresh-version-string-on-stale-code", "ci-loop-drift", "three-header-formats", "sakshi", "ecosystem.md", "version-sweep", "v6.4.79"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_incdir_marker_rides_in_band_because_both_out_of_band_channels_are_dead_on_some_target"
description = "Before v6.5.7 `cyrius build` resolved every `include` against the **process CWD**, so a source in a subfolder could not include a file sitting next to it — the limit that forced agnos into a shell workaround. The fix passes the entry file's directory to cycc as **`#@incdir <dir>` on line 1 of the source itself**, and the reason it is IN-BAND is the durable part: **both out-of-band channels are dead on some target.** `chdir` breaks the root-relative dep prepend, and `_read_env` is **stubbed on Windows and cx**. In-band works untaught everywhere because `#` opens a comment in cyrius, so older `cycc`, `cybs` and the cx/JS forks skip the line without knowing what it is. **BUT IN-BAND MEANS A HOSTILE `.cyr` CAN WRITE ONE**, and the original filing was wrong to call it a trusted channel — honouring an absolute directory would have rebuilt exactly the read-anything primitive CVE-16 removed. Two rules close it, both mutation-proven against a real escape target: the directory must be **RELATIVE and `..`-free** (rejected on leading `/`, leading `\\`, and an `X:` drive prefix — see `_pp_read_incdir` in `src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`), and it is read **ONLY at byte 0**, so a marker further down the file stays an ordinary comment. The CLI writes it AHEAD of the dep prepend precisely so byte 0 is where it lands (`cbt/build.cyr:_source_incdir`). RESOLUTION IS **CWD-FIRST**: the `_pp_incdir` retry sits AFTER every existing step in `READFILE` (`src/frontend/lex.cyr:713`), so no include that resolves today can change meaning — it can only turn an error into a success. THE COST IS STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: `cyrius build /abs/dir/x.cyr` gets no marker and keeps the old behaviour. No reach was bought with a hole. See CHANGELOG [6.5.7]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["#@incdir", "include-resolution", "in-band-marker", "hostile-source", "CVE-16", "relative-and-dotdot-free", "byte-0-only", "cwd-first-retry", "READFILE", "_read_env-stubbed-on-windows-and-cx", "chdir-breaks-dep-prepend", "lex_pp", "v6.5.7"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_check_that_reads_kernel_mode_during_preprocessing_always_reads_zero_because_the_env_is_parsed_after_PREPROCESS"
description = "v6.5.24, building the bare-metal forbidden-module check (arc deliverable #4). The natural place to reject a host-only `include` under a kernel build is the include site itself — you already have the filename and the bytes. It compiles, it runs, and it **NEVER FIRES**: `PREPROCESS(S)` is called at `src/main.cyr:1189` and `CYRIUS_KERNEL` is not read into `kernel_mode` (S+0x18FCA0) until `:1217`, so the cell is still 0 for the entire preprocessing pass. A gate written against it passes vacuously in both directions and looks like a working feature. **The general shape: a preprocessor-time check may not consult any state main.cyr sets after PREPROCESS — and the ordering is 28 lines apart in one file, invisible from the call site.** Fixed by SPLITTING the concern: the preprocessor RECORDS (`PP_IS_HOST_ONLY` scans the freshly-read bytes, bounded to 4096, so no file is opened twice; `PP_NOTE_HOST_ONLY` latches the name), and the decision happens at the END of `PARSE_PROG` — one shared site, no per-fork duplication, and it additionally catches the source `kernel;` directive, which is not known until every top-level statement is parsed. ⛔ **THE SECOND TRAP, caught only by check.sh's qemu-boot gate:** cbt prepends one `include` per `[deps].stdlib` module to EVERY build regardless of target, so this repo's own `programs/boot_serial.cyr` — a kernel with NO includes of its own — inherited `lib/fs.cyr` from the manifest and the first working version FAILED it (`FAIL: plain kernel build`). Left in, every kernel build in every repo whose manifest lists a host-only module would break, i.e. the feature would be unusable rather than useful. The fix reuses the same release's `#@srcline` marker as the attribution boundary: everything above it is cbt's prepend, everything below is the author's file, so a pull is blamed on the user only once the marker has been seen (with no marker — raw `cat | cycc` — every include is the user's). **Whose include is it** is a question a `#host_only`-style check has to answer, and the answer needs a marker that did not exist until the same release. Gate `tests/gates/platform/bare_metal_forbidden_module.sh`, 6 axes; axes 1/4/6 are the NEGATIVES that stop it degenerating into a blanket rejection of kernel builds. See CHANGELOG [6.5.24]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["bare-metal", "kernel-mode", "CYRIUS_KERNEL", "host_only", "preprocessor-ordering", "PREPROCESS-before-env-read", "vacuous-check", "PARSE_PROG", "cbt-prepend-attribution", "srcline-marker", "false-positive", "qemu-boot-gate", "deliverable-4", "v6.5.24"]

[[entries]]
name = "compensating_a_prepend_by_setting_a_line_bias_does_nothing_the_reanchor_has_already_happened"
description = "v6.5.24, fixing `<source>` diagnostics that shifted one line per cbt prepend (abaco's 18-module manifest: **+17**, pointing PAST EOF on a 23-line file, which reads as a compiler fault so the reader stops trusting line numbers entirely). `_pp_srcline_bias` already existed and `#@incdir` already set it to 1, so the obvious fix is to make the bias count ALL prepended lines. **That changes nothing measurable, and the reason is worth carrying: the bias is only ever CONSUMED by `PP_REANCHOR_SRC` when an `include` RETURNS.** cbt's prepended `include` lines all sit ABOVE the point where you learn the true offset, so the last re-anchor has already been emitted with the old bias and nothing recomputes it. A bias-only first cut reported the RAW line (8) where the user's line was 3 — WORSE than the +1 it started with. The marker has to RE-ANCHOR outright (`op = PP_REANCHOR(S, out, op, \"<source>\", 1)`), i.e. declare 'what follows is `<source>` line 1'; setting state for a later consumer that has already run is the bug. Costs ONE `#@file` map entry per compile, which the 1024-entry cap absorbs — and that cap is exactly why v6.5.19 PADS `#ifdef` blocks with newlines instead of marking them (agnosai would have needed ~1139 entries, and overflow makes `FM_FILEID` return -1, which `private` visibility is load-bearing on). ⭐ **The marker deliberately carries NO COUNT.** Having cbt declare 'I prepended N lines' makes N a hand-maintained value duplicating a derivable fact — the same shape that had silently drifted in three separate places in this one cycle (heap-map sizes vs regions, gate-script counts vs files, ERR_MSG lengths vs strings) — and it is unworkable the moment `[build].modules` is used, since those splice in whole FILES of unknown length. cycc derives the bias from the marker's own line position, so cbt counts nothing and cannot drift. ⚠ Not the remedy the filing proposed: re-emitting `#@file` stays foreclosed by v6.5.21's neutralisation of user-authored `#@file` (which closed a forged-marker bypass of `private`); `#@srcline` carries no filename and is honoured once, so it cannot re-open that hole. Gate `tests/gates/diagnostics/srcline_no_line_shift.sh` demands the SAME line at 0/6/18 modules and for a subdir entry, so a fix that merely shifts the constant by one cannot pass. See CHANGELOG [6.5.24]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["diagnostics", "line-numbers", "srcline", "PP_REANCHOR", "_pp_srcline_bias", "cbt-prepend", "deps-stdlib", "build-modules", "file-map-1024-cap", "private-FM_FILEID", "derive-never-restate", "past-EOF", "v6.5.24"]

